(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["graphs",{"l:graphs/how-do-you-turn-a-problem-into-a-graph-and-how-s":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A graph is a set of \u003cb>vertices\u003c/b> (things) and \u003cb>edges\u003c/b> (relationships between pairs of things). Modelling is the real work: decide what a vertex is, when two vertices are joined, whether an edge has a direction or a cost, and what the question asks. Store the graph as \u003cb>adjacency lists\u003c/b> unless it is dense or tiny, and do not build it at all when you can generate a vertex's neighbours on the fly (grids, puzzles).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Picture a rail map. Stations are vertices and the lines between neighbouring stations are edges. \"Can I get from Leeds to Bath?\" is reachability. \"With the fewest stops?\" is a shortest path. \"Which stations are cut off after a storm?\" is connected components. None of those questions cares how the map is drawn, only who is joined to whom.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Most problems never say \"graph\". A maze is a graph of open cells joined by single steps. A word game is a graph of words joined when they differ by one letter. A course catalogue is a graph of courses with an arrow from each prerequisite to the course that needs it. A bicycle lock is a graph of the 10,000 combinations joined by single wheel turns. Spotting the graph is usually most of the solution.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Four modelling questions\u003c/h5>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>What is a vertex?\u003c/b> The smallest piece of state the question cares about: a cell, a word, a course, a combination.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>When are two vertices joined?\u003c/b> That rule is the edge set: \"one step apart\", \"one letter different\", \"must come before\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Direction and cost?\u003c/b> \"A must come before B\" is directed (A -&gt; B). Friendship is undirected. Prices or distances are weights; equal costs mean an unweighted graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>What is asked?\u003c/b> The question picks the algorithm:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Statement\u003c/th>\u003cth>Vertices\u003c/th>\u003cth>Edges\u003c/th>\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Can computer 0 reach computer 5?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>computers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>cables, undirected\u003c/td>\u003ctd>reachable?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS or DFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fewest steps through a maze\u003c/td>\u003ctd>open cells\u003c/td>\u003ctd>moves to a neighbouring cell\u003c/td>\u003ctd>fewest edges\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>How many islands?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>land cells\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sideways neighbours\u003c/td>\u003ctd>count components\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DFS or BFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Can every course be taken?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>courses\u003c/td>\u003ctd>prerequisite -&gt; course\u003c/td>\u003ctd>does an order exist?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>topological sort\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fewest turns to open a lock\u003c/td>\u003ctd>4-digit combinations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>one wheel moved one notch\u003c/td>\u003ctd>fewest edges\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS over states\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cheapest flight route\u003c/td>\u003ctd>airports\u003c/td>\u003ctd>flights with prices\u003c/td>\u003ctd>least total weight\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dijkstra (shortest paths module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cheapest way to cable every office\u003c/td>\u003ctd>offices\u003c/td>\u003ctd>possible cables with costs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>least total to connect all\u003c/td>\u003ctd>spanning tree (union-find module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>Representations\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Adjacency lists\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>adj[u]\u003c/code> is the list of u's neighbours. An undirected edge is stored twice, once in each list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Adjacency matrix\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>m[u][v]\u003c/code> is True when u and v are joined. Good for dense graphs and constant-time edge tests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Edge list\u003c/b>: the raw pairs, as most inputs arrive. Good for sorting edges by weight (Kruskal) and little else.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Implicit graph\u003c/b>: a function that yields the neighbours of a vertex. Grids and puzzles are never built; you compute neighbours when you need them.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Adjacency lists\u003c/th>\u003cth>Matrix\u003c/th>\u003cth>Edge list\u003c/th>\u003cth>Neighbour function\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Space\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V + E)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V^2)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(E)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) plus your visited set\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>List the neighbours of u\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(deg u)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(E)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(deg u)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Is (u, v) an edge?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(deg u), O(1) with sets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(E)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>compute it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Use for\u003c/td>\u003ctd>almost everything\u003c/td>\u003ctd>dense graphs, V up to a few thousand\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sorting edges, input\u003c/td>\u003ctd>grids, puzzles, huge state spaces\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In Python, vertices numbered 0..n-1 get a list of lists. Arbitrary labels (names, tuples) get a \u003ccode>defaultdict(list)\u003c/code>, or you map each label to an integer once and use lists from then on, which is faster and smaller. Use sets of neighbours when you need fast edge tests or must ignore duplicate edges.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building adjacency lists appends each edge once (twice if undirected): O(V + E) time and space. A traversal then visits each vertex once and scans each adjacency list once, so it costs O(V + E) in total, because the lengths of all the lists add up to E (or 2E). With a matrix, finding the neighbours of one vertex scans a whole row of V cells, so a traversal costs O(V^2) even when the graph has few edges.\u003c/p>\u003cp>That difference decides feasibility. With V = 100,000 and E = 200,000, adjacency lists hold 400,000 entries; a matrix would need 10^10 cells. For implicit graphs, count states and moves instead: the lock has V = 10^4 combinations and 8 moves from each, so E = 8 * 10^4 and a BFS is instant.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Model as a graph whenever the input describes things plus a rule for which pairs are related, and the question is about reachability, distance in steps, groups, cycles or order. Watch for near misses:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>A tree given as node objects is already a graph with no cycles: recurse on it directly, no visited set needed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A grid where you may only move right or down is a DAG; counting or optimising paths there is dynamic programming, not BFS.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Different edge costs need Dijkstra; edges that only ever get added, with \"are these connected?\" queries in between, suit union-find.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build directed adjacency lists and in-degrees for four courses, given pairs \u003ccode>[course, prerequisite]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>[[1, 0], [2, 0], [3, 1], [3, 2]]\u003c/code>. The in-degree of a vertex (how many edges point into it) will drive topological sorting later.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pairs are [course, prerequisite]: the edge must point from the prerequisite to the course\u003c/p>\u003cpre>pairs   [1,0]  [2,0]  [3,1]  [3,2]\nadj     0: []   1: []   2: []   3: []\nindeg   0 0 0 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">[1, 0]: course 0 comes before course 1, so add the edge 0 -&gt; 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>adj     0: [\u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>]   1: []   2: []   3: []\nindeg   0 \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark> 0 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">[2, 0]: edge 0 -&gt; 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>adj     0: [1, \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>]   1: []   2: []   3: []\nindeg   0 1 \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark> 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">[3, 1]: edge 1 -&gt; 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>adj     0: [1, 2]   1: [\u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>]   2: []   3: []\nindeg   0 1 1 \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">[3, 2]: edge 2 -&gt; 3. Vertex 0 has in-degree 0: nothing must come before it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>adj     0: [1, 2]   1: [3]   2: [\u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>]   3: []\nindeg   \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark> 1 1 \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict\n\ndef build_adj(n, edges, directed=False):\n    \"\"\"Adjacency lists for vertices 0..n-1 from (u, v) pairs.\"\"\"\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]        # never [[]] * n: that is one shared list\n    for u, v in edges:\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        if not directed:\n            adj[v].append(u)\n    return adj\n\n\ndef build_adj_labels(edges, directed=False):\n    \"\"\"Adjacency lists keyed by any hashable label (names, tuples, strings).\"\"\"\n    adj = defaultdict(list)\n    for u, v in edges:\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        if not directed:\n            adj[v].append(u)\n    return adj\n\n\nDIRS = ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1))\n\ndef grid_neighbours(grid, r, c):\n    \"\"\"Implicit graph: the open cells next to (r, c); '#' is a wall.\"\"\"\n    for dr, dc in DIRS:\n        nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n        if 0 &lt;= nr &lt; len(grid) and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; len(grid[0]) and grid[nr][nc] != '#':\n            yield nr, nc\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>[[]] * n\u003c/code>\u003c/b> creates n references to one list, so every vertex shares one neighbour list. Use a comprehension.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Backwards edges.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>[course, prerequisite]\u003c/code> is a pair, not an edge. Write the direction in a comment before coding.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the reverse edge\u003c/b> for undirected input, so traversals miss half the graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>defaultdict surprises.\u003c/b> Vertices with no edges never become keys, so \u003ccode>for u in adj\u003c/code> misses them; and merely reading \u003ccode>adj[x]\u003c/code> inserts x, which raises \"dictionary changed size during iteration\" inside a loop over \u003ccode>adj\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1-based labels\u003c/b> (common on CSES): allocate \u003ccode>n + 1\u003c/code> lists or subtract one on input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Parallel edges and self-loops\u003c/b> inflate degrees; use sets if the problem says they should count once.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Trace \u003ccode>build_adj(3, [(0, 1), (0, 2), (2, 1)], directed=True)\u003c/code>. What are the lists and the in-degrees?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>adj = [[1, 2], [], [1]]\u003c/code>. In-degrees are 0 for vertex 0, 2 for vertex 1 (from 0 and 2) and 1 for vertex 2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a traversal cost O(V + E) with adjacency lists but O(V^2) with a matrix, even on the same sparse graph?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A traversal looks at the neighbours of every vertex once. With lists that work is the sum of the list lengths, which is E (2E undirected), plus O(1) per vertex. With a matrix, finding the neighbours of one vertex means reading its whole row of V cells, whether or not they are edges, so V rows cost V^2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A graph has 2,000 vertices and 1.5 million edges, and your algorithm asks \"is u joined to v?\" millions of times. Which representation?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An adjacency matrix: 4 million cells fit easily, the graph is dense (E is close to V^2 / 2 = 2 million), and each test is O(1). Lists would make each test O(deg u), hundreds of steps. A list of sets also gives O(1) average tests at higher memory per edge.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague writes \u003ccode>adj = [[]] * n\u003c/code> and then \u003ccode>adj[u].append(v)\u003c/code> for each edge. What does a BFS from vertex 0 then see?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every \u003ccode>adj[i]\u003c/code> is the same list, so every vertex appears to be joined to every endpoint of every edge. BFS reaches vertices that are not connected to 0 and reports wrong distances.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1971 Find if Path Exists in Graph.\u003c/b> Build undirected adjacency lists from the edge list, then run any traversal from \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>. Expected: O(V + E) time and space; for n = 6, edges \u003ccode>[[0,1],[1,2],[3,4],[4,5]]\u003c/code> and source 0, destination 2 gives \u003ccode>true\u003c/code> and destination 5 gives \u003ccode>false\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Model it (no code).\u003c/b> A knight starts on a1 of a chessboard and wants to reach h8 in the fewest moves. Name the vertices, the edges and the algorithm. Expected: 64 vertices (squares), an edge for each legal knight move (336 ordered moves, so 168 undirected edges), BFS from a1; the answer is 6 moves.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to read a statement as vertices and edges, choose a representation by its costs, and match the question to a traversal.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/how-does-bfs-find-the-shortest-path-in-an-unweig":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Breadth-first search explores in rings: the source, then everything one edge away, then everything two edges away. A first-in first-out queue keeps the rings in order, so the \u003cb>first time you reach a vertex you have found a shortest path\u003c/b> to it (fewest edges). Set \u003ccode>dist[v]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>parent[v]\u003c/code> \u003cb>when you enqueue v\u003c/b>, never later, and each vertex enters the queue once: O(V + E) time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Drop a stone in a pond. The ripple reaches every point at distance 1 before any point at distance 2. BFS is that ripple on a graph: your friends, then friends of friends, then the next ring. The ring a person first appears in is their degree of separation from you.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Put the source in a queue with \u003ccode>dist[source] = 0\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pop the oldest vertex u. For each neighbour v that has no distance yet, set \u003ccode>dist[v] = dist[u] + 1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>parent[v] = u\u003c/code>, and push v.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Repeat until the queue is empty. To rebuild the path to t, follow \u003ccode>parent\u003c/code> from t back to the source and reverse.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>\u003cb>Invariant:\u003c/b> the queue always holds vertices in non-decreasing order of distance, and at most two distances are present at once: some d at the front and d + 1 behind them. Popping a vertex at distance d only ever appends vertices at distance d + 1, so the order is kept.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why the first discovery is shortest.\u003c/b> Prove by induction on k that every vertex whose true distance is k gets \u003ccode>dist = k\u003c/code>, and that all of them are popped before any vertex at true distance k + 1. For k = 0 it is the source. Take a vertex v at true distance k + 1. Some neighbour w of v has true distance k, so w is popped before any distance-(k + 1) vertex, and when it is popped it discovers v if nobody has yet. Could something discover v earlier? Only a vertex popped earlier, which has true distance at most k; if its distance were less than k, v would be closer than k + 1. So v's discoverer is at distance exactly k and \u003ccode>dist[v] = k + 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Mark when you enqueue\u003c/h5>\u003cp>If you mark a vertex visited only when you pop it, every neighbour that sees it before then pushes it again. On a complete graph of 1,000 vertices that makes 499,501 pushes instead of 1,000; on an open 100 x 100 grid, 19,801 instead of 10,000. Distances can still come out right if you skip repeats when popping, but time and memory balloon and parent pointers get overwritten. Mark at discovery and the problem disappears.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each vertex is pushed at most once (its distance is set on the push) and popped at most once: O(V) queue work, each O(1) with \u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code>. Each adjacency list is scanned once, when its vertex is popped, and the lengths sum to E (2E undirected). Total \u003cb>O(V + E) time and O(V) extra space\u003c/b> for \u003ccode>dist\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>parent\u003c/code> and the queue. With \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code> each pop shifts the whole list, which makes BFS O(V^2) on large graphs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Fewest edges, moves, hops or transformations, when every step costs the same.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Anything by levels: \"within k steps\", \"nearest first\", \"spread one ring per minute\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>Not with different edge weights: a path with more, cheaper edges can win, so you need Dijkstra (or 0-1 BFS when weights are only 0 and 1), both in the shortest paths module.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For plain reachability or counting components, BFS and DFS are equally good; DFS is shorter to write recursively and gives post-order, BFS never hits a recursion limit.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>BFS from A on this graph (neighbours are scanned in alphabetical order):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>    A --- B --- E\n    |     |     |\n    C --- D --- F --- G\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start: A has distance 0 and is the only vertex in the queue\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [A]\ndist    A=0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop A. B and C are new: label them 1, record parent A, enqueue\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [\u003cmark>B\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>C\u003c/mark>]\ndist    A=0  \u003cmark>B=1\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>C=1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop B. A is labelled; D and E are new at distance 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [C, \u003cmark>D\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>E\u003c/mark>]\ndist    A=0  B=1  C=1  \u003cmark>D=2\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>E=2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop C. A and D already have labels (D was marked when it was enqueued), so nothing is added\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [D, E]\ndist    A=0  B=1  C=1  D=2  E=2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop D. F is new at distance 3, parent D\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [E, \u003cmark>F\u003c/mark>]\ndist    ...  D=2  E=2  \u003cmark>F=3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop E. F already has a label from D, so E adds nothing\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [F]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop F. G is new at distance 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue   [\u003cmark>G\u003c/mark>]\ndist    ...  F=3  \u003cmark>G=4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop G; the queue is empty. Path to G from parents: G &lt;- F &lt;- D &lt;- B &lt;- A\u003c/p>\u003cpre>path    A -&gt; B -&gt; D -&gt; F -&gt; G   (4 edges)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef bfs(adj, source):\n    \"\"\"Fewest-edge distances and BFS-tree parents from source (unweighted graph).\"\"\"\n    dist = {source: 0}\n    parent = {source: None}\n    queue = deque([source])\n    while queue:\n        u = queue.popleft()\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            if v not in dist:              # first discovery is a shortest path\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1      # mark when you enqueue\n                parent[v] = u\n                queue.append(v)\n    return dist, parent\n\n\ndef path_to(parent, target):\n    \"\"\"Vertices from the source to target via parent pointers; [] if target was not reached.\"\"\"\n    if target not in parent:\n        return []\n    path = []\n    while target is not None:\n        path.append(target)\n        target = parent[target]\n    return path[::-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>adj\u003c/code> can be a list of lists or a dict of lists. For level-by-level work (minutes, rounds), process \u003ccode>for _ in range(len(queue))\u003c/code> pops per round, or keep the current level in a list and build the next one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Marking on pop\u003c/b> instead of on push (see above).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code>\u003c/b>: O(n) per pop. Use \u003ccode>deque.popleft()\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Using 0 as \"unvisited\"\u003c/b> in a distance array: the source has distance 0. Use -1, \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>in\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting the wrong thing\u003c/b>: some problems count vertices on the path (edges + 1), for example a grid path \"of length k cells\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unreachable targets\u003c/b>: decide what to return (-1, \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>) before coding.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Weighted edges\u003c/b>: BFS silently returns the path with fewest edges, not the cheapest.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Add an edge C - G to the trace graph. What are \u003ccode>dist[G]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>parent[G]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>dist[F]\u003c/code> now?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When C is popped (third pop), G is new, so \u003ccode>dist[G] = 2\u003c/code> with parent C. The queue is then [D, E, G]; D is popped before G and discovers F at distance 3, so \u003ccode>dist[F] = 3\u003c/code> with parent D, unchanged. The path to G becomes A -&gt; C -&gt; G.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must the container be first-in first-out? Give a graph where a stack gives a wrong distance.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A FIFO queue pops all distance-d vertices before any at d + 1, which the proof needs. With a stack, take edges A-B, A-C, C-D, D-X, B-X and neighbours in that order. Popping A labels B and C as 1; the stack pops C first and labels D as 2; then pops D and labels X as 3. But X is at distance 2 via B, and B is never given the chance to discover it first.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You need the fewest knight moves between two squares on a 1,000 x 1,000 board, and separately the cheapest flight between two airports. Which uses BFS?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The knight: every move costs the same, so BFS over squares (10^6 vertices, 8 edges each) is right. Flights have prices, so the fewest flights may not be the cheapest; use Dijkstra.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A BFS marks a vertex as seen only when it pops it: it pushes every neighbour that has not been popped yet, and skips a popped vertex that was already seen. Is it correct? How many pushes on a complete graph of n vertices?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Distances come out right if they are recorded when a vertex is first popped, but every vertex is pushed once for each neighbour popped before it, about n^2 / 2 pushes (499,501 for n = 1,000) instead of n. The queue can hold O(E) entries instead of O(V), and the time grows with it. Mark on push.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1971 Find if Path Exists in Graph.\u003c/b> Run \u003ccode>bfs\u003c/code> from \u003ccode>source\u003c/code> and check whether \u003ccode>destination\u003c/code> got a distance (or stop early when you reach it). Expected: O(V + E); for n = 6, edges \u003ccode>[[0,1],[1,2],[3,4],[4,5]]\u003c/code>, source 0: destination 2 is reachable, destination 5 is not.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1091 Shortest Path in Binary Matrix.\u003c/b> BFS over open cells with eight directions; the answer counts cells, so start at 1. Expected: O(n^2) time and space; \u003ccode>[[0,0,0],[1,1,0],[1,1,0]]\u003c/code> gives 4, and \u003ccode>[[1,0],[0,0]]\u003c/code> gives -1 because the start is blocked.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find fewest-edge paths with BFS, rebuild them from parents, and argue why the first discovery is the shortest.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/how-does-dfs-find-connected-components-and-when-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Depth-first search follows one path as far as it can, then backs up to the most recent vertex with an unexplored edge. Started from every vertex not yet seen, each start discovers exactly one \u003cb>connected component\u003c/b>. The recursive version is the shortest to write; the \u003cb>iterative version with an explicit stack\u003c/b> is the one to use when the graph can be deep, because Python stops at 1,000 nested calls by default.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Explore a cave with a piece of chalk. At every junction take a passage you have not chalked yet; when every passage from here is chalked, walk back the way you came and try again from there. When you are back at the entrance with nothing left, you have seen every chamber connected to it. To count separate cave systems, go to an entrance you have not chalked and start again: each fresh start is a new system.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Recursive DFS and components\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>dfs(u)\u003c/code> marks u, then calls itself on each unmarked neighbour. The call stack is the path from the start to the current vertex. A driver loop starts a new search from every unmarked vertex and counts how many searches it started.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two orders fall out of the recursion and matter later:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Preorder\u003c/b>: when a vertex is entered (marked). Useful for copying or printing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Postorder\u003c/b>: when a vertex is left, after all its descendants. A vertex finishes only after everything reachable from it through unvisited vertices has finished. Cycle detection and topological sorting are built on this order.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Iterative DFS\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Replace the call stack with a list. Pop a vertex, push its unmarked neighbours, and \u003cb>mark them as you push\u003c/b> so nothing is pushed twice. This visits every reachable vertex exactly once, which is all components and flood fill need. It does not reproduce the recursive preorder (on the trace graph below it visits A, C, D, F, G, E, B), and it has no natural \"leave\" moment; when you need postorder, keep an iterator per stack frame, as in the cycle-detection card.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Flood fill\u003c/h5>\u003cp>On a grid the same search recolours a region: start at a cell, and repeatedly step to sideways neighbours that still have the old colour. Recolouring a cell doubles as marking it visited, so no separate set is needed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every vertex is marked once and every adjacency list is scanned once: \u003cb>O(V + E) time\u003c/b>. Space is O(V) for the marks plus the stack. For recursion that stack is the depth of the search, which can be V: a path graph, or a snake-shaped region in a grid, recurses once per vertex.\u003c/p>\u003cp>CPython's default limit is 1,000 frames (\u003ccode>sys.getrecursionlimit()\u003c/code>). A 1,000 x 1,000 all-land grid can recurse a million deep and raises \u003ccode>RecursionError\u003c/code> long before. \u003ccode>sys.setrecursionlimit\u003c/code> raises the ceiling, and since CPython 3.11 pure Python calls no longer use the C stack, so a high limit usually works; on older versions it can crash the interpreter, and deep recursion is slower than a loop either way. The explicit stack has no such limit.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Need\u003c/th>\u003cth>BFS\u003c/th>\u003cth>DFS\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fewest edges to a target\u003c/td>\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>no\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reachability, components, flood fill\u003c/td>\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Postorder: directed cycles, topological order, subtree totals\u003c/td>\u003ctd>no\u003c/td>\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the widest level\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the deepest path\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Python recursion limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>not an issue\u003c/td>\u003ctd>use the iterative form or raise the limit\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Every path, with choices undone (backtracking)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>no\u003c/td>\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Choose DFS when you need to visit everything and do not care about distance, or when you need finishing times. When edges arrive one at a time with connectivity questions in between, union-find (next module) beats running DFS again after every edge.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Recursive DFS from A on the graph from the BFS card (neighbours in alphabetical order). The stack shows the active calls.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>    A --- B --- E\n    |     |     |\n    C --- D --- F --- G\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Call dfs(A): mark A. Its first neighbour B is unmarked\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [A]\npre     A\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">dfs(B): A is marked, D is not, so go deeper before looking at E\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [A, \u003cmark>B\u003c/mark>]\npre     A B\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">dfs(D): B is marked, C is not\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [A, B, \u003cmark>D\u003c/mark>]\npre     A B D\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">dfs(C): both neighbours (A, D) are marked, so C finishes first\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [A, B, D, \u003cmark>C\u003c/mark>]\npre     A B D C\npost    \u003cmark>C\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Back in D: next neighbour F is unmarked; F's first unmarked neighbour is E, which finishes at once\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [A, B, D, F, \u003cmark>E\u003c/mark>]\npre     A B D C F E\npost    C \u003cmark>E\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Back in F: G is unmarked; G has no other neighbours and finishes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   [A, B, D, F, \u003cmark>G\u003c/mark>]\npre     A B D C F E G\npost    C E \u003cmark>G\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">F, D, B and A have no unmarked neighbours left and finish in turn; one component of 7 vertices\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack   []\npost    C E G \u003cmark>F D B A\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_components(n, adj):\n    \"\"\"Number of connected components of an undirected graph on 0..n-1 (recursive DFS).\"\"\"\n    seen = [False] * n\n\n    def dfs(u):\n        seen[u] = True\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            if not seen[v]:\n                dfs(v)\n\n    count = 0\n    for s in range(n):\n        if not seen[s]:\n            dfs(s)\n            count += 1\n    return count\n\n\ndef component_labels(n, adj):\n    \"\"\"Component number of every vertex, with an explicit stack (safe for deep graphs).\"\"\"\n    label = [-1] * n\n    count = 0\n    for s in range(n):\n        if label[s] != -1:\n            continue\n        label[s] = count\n        stack = [s]\n        while stack:\n            u = stack.pop()\n            for v in adj[u]:\n                if label[v] == -1:\n                    label[v] = count        # mark on push: each vertex is pushed once\n                    stack.append(v)\n        count += 1\n    return label\n\n\ndef flood_fill(grid, r, c, new):\n    \"\"\"Recolour the 4-connected region of equal values containing (r, c), in place.\"\"\"\n    old = grid[r][c]\n    if old == new:\n        return grid                         # otherwise the loop below never ends\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    grid[r][c] = new\n    stack = [(r, c)]\n    while stack:\n        i, j = stack.pop()\n        for ni, nj in ((i + 1, j), (i - 1, j), (i, j + 1), (i, j - 1)):\n            if 0 &lt;= ni &lt; rows and 0 &lt;= nj &lt; cols and grid[ni][nj] == old:\n                grid[ni][nj] = new          # the new colour is the visited mark\n                stack.append((ni, nj))\n    return grid\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recursion depth\u003c/b> on grids and long chains: use the iterative version, or BFS.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Marking on pop in the iterative version\u003c/b>: a vertex can then sit on the stack many times. Mark on push, or skip already-marked vertices when popping.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Flood fill with the same colour\u003c/b>: if \u003ccode>new == old\u003c/code>, recolouring never changes anything, so the region is pushed forever. Return early.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mutating the caller's grid\u003c/b> when they did not expect it; copy first if the input must survive.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Strings are immutable\u003c/b>: a grid given as a list of strings must become a list of lists before you recolour it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Diagonal neighbours\u003c/b>: only include them when the statement says cells touching at corners are connected.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the trace graph, scan B's neighbours in the order E, D, A instead (all other lists alphabetical). What are the preorder and postorder from A?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Preorder A, B, E, F, D, C, G. Postorder C, D, G, F, E, B, A. From B the search goes to E first, then F, then D (F's first neighbour), then C; G is reached from F after D finishes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does each start in the driver loop discover exactly one component?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>DFS from s marks every vertex reachable from s through unmarked vertices. Nothing in s's component was marked before (otherwise s would have been marked too, since marks spread along all edges of a component), so the search marks the whole component and nothing outside it, because no edge leaves a component.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You must count islands in a 2,000 x 2,000 grid that may be almost entirely land. Recursive DFS, iterative DFS or BFS?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Iterative DFS or BFS. A single island can contain 4 million cells, and recursive DFS can nest once per cell, far beyond the default limit of 1,000 frames. Both alternatives keep their own stack or queue on the heap and run in O(rows x cols).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A flood fill recolours by pushing neighbours with \u003ccode>grid[ni][nj] == old\u003c/code> and sets the colour only when a cell is popped. What goes wrong?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A cell keeps the old colour while it waits on the stack, so other neighbours push it again; it can be pushed up to four times, wasting time and memory. Recolour (mark) at push time, as in the template.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>733 Flood Fill.\u003c/b> Iterative or recursive fill from the start pixel, with the early return when the colour is unchanged. Expected: O(m n) time; for image \u003ccode>[[1,1,0],[1,0,0],[0,1,1]]\u003c/code>, start (0, 0) and colour 2 the result is \u003ccode>[[2,2,0],[2,0,0],[0,1,1]]\u003c/code> (the 1s at the bottom right are not connected); with colour 1 the image is returned unchanged.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>200 Number of Islands.\u003c/b> Scan every cell; each unvisited \u003ccode>'1'\u003c/code> starts a fill and adds one island. Expected: O(m n) time; for rows \u003ccode>\"10101\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>\"11001\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>\"00011\"\u003c/code> the answer is 3 (cells touching only at a corner are separate islands).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count components and fill regions with DFS, read pre- and postorder, and keep deep searches off the call stack.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/how-do-you-run-bfs-on-a-grid-and-why-start-from-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A grid is a graph you never build: each open cell is a vertex and its neighbours come from a \u003cb>direction array\u003c/b> plus a \u003cb>bounds check\u003c/b>. To find, for every cell, the distance to the \u003cb>nearest\u003c/b> of many sources, put \u003cb>all the sources in the queue at distance 0\u003c/b> and run one BFS: O(rows x cols) instead of one search per source. When every cell asks \"can I reach the edge?\", turn it around and search once \u003cb>from\u003c/b> the edge.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Fires break out in three places at the same moment and spread one cell per minute. Each cell catches fire at the minute the nearest fire reaches it. You do not simulate each fire separately and take the minimum; you let all three spread together, ring by ring. That is multi-source BFS.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Another way to see it: invent a \"super source\" joined to every real source by an edge. BFS from the super source puts all real sources at distance 1, then their neighbours at 2, and so on. Subtract one and you have the distance to the nearest source. Seeding the queue with all sources at distance 0 is exactly that, without the extra vertex.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Grid conventions\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>rows = len(grid)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cols = len(grid[0])\u003c/code>; a cell is \u003ccode>(r, c)\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>DIRS = ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1))\u003c/code> for 4-neighbours; add the four diagonals for 8.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check bounds \u003cb>before\u003c/b> indexing. In Python \u003ccode>grid[-1]\u003c/code> is silently the last row, so a missing check wraps around instead of failing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The distance array doubles as the visited set: -1 means unseen.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Multi-source BFS\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Initialise every source with distance 0 and push them all, in any order. The queue then starts as one complete \"level 0\", and the usual BFS invariant holds: levels come out in order. The first time a cell is reached, it is reached from its nearest source. If the question is in rounds (\"minutes until all oranges rot\"), process the queue one level at a time and count levels.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Reversing the search\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\"Which cells can reach the border?\" asked from every cell costs a BFS per cell. Ask instead \"which cells can the border reach, walking the moves backwards?\" and one search answers for everyone. On undirected grids the moves are symmetric, so you just start at the border cells. When moves are directed (water flows only to a cell that is not higher), walk the reverse rule: from a cell you may step to a neighbour that is \u003cb>not lower\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each cell is pushed once, when its distance is set, and popped once; each pop checks 4 (or 8) neighbours: \u003cb>O(rows x cols) time and space\u003c/b>, however many sources there are. Running one BFS per source instead costs O(S x rows x cols): on a 1,000 x 1,000 grid with 1,000 sources that is 10^9 cell visits against 10^6.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Speed tips for Python: store distances in a list of lists (or a flat list indexed by \u003ccode>r * cols + c\u003c/code>) rather than a dict of tuples, and hoist \u003ccode>rows\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cols\u003c/code> and the direction tuple out of the loop.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\"Distance from every cell to the nearest X\", \"time until everything is infected, burnt or rotten\", \"distance to the nearest exit or border\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>Several starting points that act simultaneously.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\"Which cells can reach the ocean, the border or the exit\": search from the target side once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Not when sources start at different times or cells cost different amounts to enter: that is Dijkstra with several starting distances (shortest paths module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Not when only one source matters: plain BFS.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Single source first. BFS from S on a 4 x 5 grid (\u003ccode>#\u003c/code> is a wall). Each step is one level; the highlighted cells get their distance \u003cb>when they are enqueued\u003c/b>, so no cell can enter the queue twice.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 0: the start cell\u003c/p>\u003cpre>|\u003cmark> 0\u003c/mark>  .  .  #  .|\n| .  #  .  .  .|\n| .  #  .  #  .|\n| .  .  .  #  .|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 1: both open neighbours of S\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0 \u003cmark> 1\u003c/mark>  .  #  .|\n|\u003cmark> 1\u003c/mark>  #  .  .  .|\n| .  #  .  #  .|\n| .  .  .  #  .|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0  1 \u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>  #  .|\n| 1  #  .  .  .|\n|\u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>  #  .  #  .|\n| .  .  .  #  .|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 3: the two fronts move around the wall separately\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0  1  2  #  .|\n| 1  # \u003cmark> 3\u003c/mark>  .  .|\n| 2  #  .  #  .|\n|\u003cmark> 3\u003c/mark>  .  .  #  .|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 4: the upper front labels (1, 3) and (2, 2), the lower front labels (3, 1) (cells are (row, column) from 0)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0  1  2  #  .|\n| 1  #  3 \u003cmark> 4\u003c/mark>  .|\n| 2  # \u003cmark> 4\u003c/mark>  #  .|\n| 3 \u003cmark> 4\u003c/mark>  .  #  .|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 5: (3, 1) is popped first and labels (3, 2). When (2, 2) is popped next, (3, 2) already has a label, so it is not queued a second time\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0  1  2  #  .|\n| 1  #  3  4 \u003cmark> 5\u003c/mark>|\n| 2  #  4  #  .|\n| 3  4 \u003cmark> 5\u003c/mark>  #  .|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Levels 6 and 7: the right-hand column fills downwards; the bottom-right cell is 7 steps away\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0  1  2  #  6|\n| 1  #  3  4  5|\n| 2  #  4  #  6|\n| 3  4  5  # \u003cmark> 7\u003c/mark>|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Now three sources (S) at once on a 5 x 7 grid. Every cell ends up labelled with the distance to its nearest S.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 0: all three sources are in the queue before the loop starts\u003c/p>\u003cpre>|\u003cmark> 0\u003c/mark>  .  .  .  #  .  .|\n| .  #  #  .  #  .  .|\n| .  .  .  .  .  . \u003cmark> 0\u003c/mark>|\n| #  .  #  #  .  .  .|\n|\u003cmark> 0\u003c/mark>  .  .  .  .  .  .|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 1: each source labels its own neighbours\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0 \u003cmark> 1\u003c/mark>  .  .  #  .  .|\n|\u003cmark> 1\u003c/mark>  #  #  .  #  . \u003cmark> 1\u003c/mark>|\n| .  .  .  .  . \u003cmark> 1\u003c/mark>  0|\n| #  .  #  #  .  . \u003cmark> 1\u003c/mark>|\n| 0 \u003cmark> 1\u003c/mark>  .  .  .  .  .|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 2: the fronts grow; a cell already labelled is never relabelled\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0  1 \u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>  .  #  . \u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>|\n| 1  #  #  .  # \u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>  1|\n|\u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>  .  .  . \u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>  1  0|\n| # \u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>  #  #  . \u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>  1|\n| 0  1 \u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>  .  .  . \u003cmark> 2\u003c/mark>|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 3: fronts from different sources meet; each cell keeps the first (smallest) label\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0  1  2 \u003cmark> 3\u003c/mark>  # \u003cmark> 3\u003c/mark>  2|\n| 1  #  #  .  #  2  1|\n| 2 \u003cmark> 3\u003c/mark>  . \u003cmark> 3\u003c/mark>  2  1  0|\n| #  2  #  # \u003cmark> 3\u003c/mark>  2  1|\n| 0  1  2 \u003cmark> 3\u003c/mark>  . \u003cmark> 3\u003c/mark>  2|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 4: the last open cells; one pass labelled all 28 open cells\u003c/p>\u003cpre>| 0  1  2  3  #  3  2|\n| 1  #  # \u003cmark> 4\u003c/mark>  #  2  1|\n| 2  3 \u003cmark> 4\u003c/mark>  3  2  1  0|\n| #  2  #  #  3  2  1|\n| 0  1  2  3 \u003cmark> 4\u003c/mark>  3  2|\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef grid_bfs(grid, sources):\n    \"\"\"Steps from the nearest source to every open cell ('#' is a wall); -1 if unreachable.\"\"\"\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    dist = [[-1] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    queue = deque()\n    for r, c in sources:\n        dist[r][c] = 0\n        queue.append((r, c))\n    while queue:\n        r, c = queue.popleft()\n        for dr, dc in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 &lt;= nr &lt; rows and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; cols and grid[nr][nc] != '#' and dist[nr][nc] == -1:\n                dist[nr][nc] = dist[r][c] + 1      # mark on enqueue\n                queue.append((nr, nc))\n    return dist\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A single-source search is \u003ccode>grid_bfs(grid, [start])\u003c/code>. For rounds, wrap the body of the loop in \u003ccode>for _ in range(len(queue)):\u003c/code> and count the rounds that changed something.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Bounds after indexing\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>grid[nr][nc] != '#' and 0 &lt;= nr &lt; rows\u003c/code> reads \u003ccode>grid[-1]\u003c/code> (wraps) or raises \u003ccode>IndexError\u003c/code> before the check runs. Check bounds first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting rounds off by one\u003c/b>: the last round may add nothing. Count only rounds that changed a cell, or stop when nothing is left to change.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unreachable targets\u003c/b>: after the search, cells still at -1 (or fresh oranges still fresh) mean \"impossible\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Duplicate sources\u003c/b>: harmless if you label before pushing and skip already-labelled cells.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Searching from the wrong side\u003c/b>: if many cells ask the same question about a fixed target set, start at the targets.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the multi-source trace, what is the distance of the cell in row 2, column 1 (counting from 0), and which source gives it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>3, set at level 3. Two sources tie: the top-left S reaches it in three steps (down, down, right) and the bottom-left S also in three (right, up, up). BFS does not care which one labels it first; the distance is the same.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does seeding several sources at distance 0 give each cell the distance to its nearest source?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is ordinary BFS from an imaginary super source joined to every real source, with every distance reduced by one. BFS from one source returns shortest distances, and the shortest path from the super source to a cell goes through the nearest real source.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For every cell of a height map you must decide whether water can flow (to equal or lower neighbours) to the top border. What do you search, and from where?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One search from all top-border cells, stepping from a cell to a neighbour whose height is greater than or equal to the current one (the reverse of the flow rule). Every cell reached can drain to the top border. That is O(rows x cols) instead of one search per cell.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague's BFS counts minutes by incrementing after every round and returns the count, but gets 1 too many on a grid where everything rots. Why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The final round pops the last cells to rot and finds nothing new, but still increments the counter. Stop when no fresh cells remain (\u003ccode>while queue and fresh:\u003c/code>), or count only rounds that changed something.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>994 Rotting Oranges.\u003c/b> Multi-source BFS seeded with every rotten orange, one level per minute, and a count of fresh oranges left. Expected: O(m n); for \u003ccode>[[2,1,1,0],[0,1,0,1],[1,1,1,2]]\u003c/code> the answer is 3; for \u003ccode>[[2,1,0],[0,0,1]]\u003c/code> it is -1 (the last orange is cut off); for \u003ccode>[[0,2]]\u003c/code> it is 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>417 Pacific Atlantic Water Flow.\u003c/b> Reverse the flow: search uphill (to neighbours that are not lower) from the Pacific edges (top row, left column) and separately from the Atlantic edges (bottom row, right column); answer the cells in both sets. Expected: O(m n); for heights \u003ccode>[[3,3,3],[3,1,3],[0,2,4]]\u003c/code> every cell except the centre pit (1, 1) qualifies, 8 cells in total.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to treat a grid as an implicit graph, label every cell with its distance to the nearest source in one pass, and search backwards from targets.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:graphs/maze-lab-bfs-dfs-and-distance-maps":"\u003cp>You will carve mazes with an explicit-stack DFS, solve them with BFS and with DFS, look at what each one explored, check BFS distances against a slow oracle that uses no queue at all, and time BFS as the maze grows. Everything uses the standard library.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>maze_lab.py\u003c/code>. The grid has walls (\u003ccode>#\u003c/code>) between cells; cell (r, c) of the maze sits at grid position (2r + 1, 2c + 1), and the border is solid, so neighbours never fall off the grid.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\nimport time\nfrom collections import deque\n\nOPEN, WALL = ' ', '#'\nSTEPS = ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1))\n\n\ndef make_maze(h, w, loops=0.0, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"A (2h+1) x (2w+1) grid carved by randomised DFS; `loops` knocks out extra walls.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    H, W = 2 * h + 1, 2 * w + 1\n    g = [[WALL] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    g[1][1] = OPEN\n    seen = {(0, 0)}\n    stack = [(0, 0)]\n    while stack:\n        r, c = stack[-1]\n        fresh = [(r + dr, c + dc) for dr, dc in STEPS\n                 if 0 &lt;= r + dr &lt; h and 0 &lt;= c + dc &lt; w and (r + dr, c + dc) not in seen]\n        if not fresh:\n            stack.pop()                                  # dead end: back up\n            continue\n        nr, nc = rng.choice(fresh)\n        g[r + nr + 1][c + nc + 1] = OPEN                 # knock down the wall between the cells\n        g[2 * nr + 1][2 * nc + 1] = OPEN\n        seen.add((nr, nc))\n        stack.append((nr, nc))\n    for _ in range(int(loops * h * w)):\n        r, c = rng.randrange(1, H - 1), rng.randrange(1, W - 1)\n        if (r + c) % 2 == 1:                             # a wall between two cells, not a pillar\n            g[r][c] = OPEN\n    return g\n\n\ndef solve(g, start, goal, frontier=\"bfs\"):\n    \"\"\"Path start..goal, cells taken off the frontier (queue or stack), and the parent map.\"\"\"\n    parent = {start: None}\n    bag = deque([start])\n    taken = 0\n    while bag:\n        cell = bag.popleft() if frontier == \"bfs\" else bag.pop()\n        taken += 1\n        if cell == goal:\n            break\n        r, c = cell\n        for dr, dc in STEPS:\n            nxt = (r + dr, c + dc)\n            if g[nxt[0]][nxt[1]] == OPEN and nxt not in parent:\n                parent[nxt] = cell\n                bag.append(nxt)\n    path, cell = [], goal\n    while cell is not None:\n        path.append(cell)\n        cell = parent[cell]\n    return path[::-1], taken, parent\n\n\ndef draw(g, path, explored):\n    rows = [row[:] for row in g]\n    for r, c in explored:\n        rows[r][c] = '.'\n    for r, c in path:\n        rows[r][c] = 'o'\n    return \"\\n\".join(\"\".join(row) for row in rows)\n\n\ndef distance_map(g, sources):\n    \"\"\"Multi-source BFS: steps from every open cell to its nearest source.\"\"\"\n    dist = {s: 0 for s in sources}\n    queue = deque(sources)\n    while queue:\n        r, c = queue.popleft()\n        for dr, dc in STEPS:\n            nxt = (r + dr, c + dc)\n            if g[nxt[0]][nxt[1]] == OPEN and nxt not in dist:\n                dist[nxt] = dist[(r, c)] + 1\n                queue.append(nxt)\n    return dist\n\n\ndef slow_distances(g, sources):\n    \"\"\"Oracle: relax every cell from its neighbours until nothing changes (no queue at all).\"\"\"\n    cells = [(r, c) for r in range(len(g)) for c in range(len(g[0])) if g[r][c] == OPEN]\n    dist = {cell: float('inf') for cell in cells}\n    for s in sources:\n        dist[s] = 0\n    changed = True\n    while changed:\n        changed = False\n        for r, c in cells:\n            for dr, dc in STEPS:\n                nb = (r + dr, c + dc)\n                if nb in dist and dist[nb] + 1 &lt; dist[(r, c)]:\n                    dist[(r, c)] = dist[nb] + 1\n                    changed = True\n    return {k: v for k, v in dist.items() if v != float('inf')}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Solve one small maze both ways and look at the pictures (\u003ccode>o\u003c/code> is the path, \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> is every cell the search discovered):\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>g = make_maze(6, 12, seed=1)\nstart, goal = (1, 1), (len(g) - 2, len(g[0]) - 2)\nfor kind in (\"bfs\", \"dfs\"):\n    path, taken, parent = solve(g, start, goal, kind)\n    print(kind, \"path cells:\", len(path), \"taken off the frontier:\", taken)\n    print(draw(g, path, parent))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Compare BFS and DFS on 20 mazes of 40 x 40 cells, as perfect mazes (\u003ccode>loops=0\u003c/code>) and with extra openings (\u003ccode>loops=0.1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>0.3\u003c/code>). For each setting, average the path length and the number of cells taken off the frontier, and put them in a table.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stress-test against the oracle on 200 small random mazes with one to three random sources: \u003ccode>distance_map\u003c/code> must equal \u003ccode>slow_distances\u003c/code>; the BFS path from \u003ccode>solve\u003c/code> must have exactly \u003ccode>distance_map(g, [start])[goal] + 1\u003c/code> cells; the DFS path must be a valid walk (consecutive cells adjacent) and never shorter than the BFS path. Stop at the first mismatch and print the maze.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Draw a distance map from two exits (top left and bottom right) of \u003ccode>make_maze(5, 10, seed=3)\u003c/code>, printing \u003ccode>dist % 10\u003c/code> in each open cell. Before running, predict where the largest digits will be.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Doubling experiment: build \u003ccode>make_maze(side, side, 0.1, 0)\u003c/code> for side 100, 200, 400 and 800, and time \u003ccode>distance_map(g, [(1, 1)])\u003c/code> (best of three) with \u003ccode>time.perf_counter()\u003c/code>. Compute the ratio between consecutive times.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional, a preview of A*: replace the deque in \u003ccode>solve\u003c/code> with a heap ordered by Manhattan distance to the goal (greedy best-first search) and compare the cells taken and the path length with BFS on mazes with loops.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>In the small maze, BFS and DFS draw the \u003cb>same path\u003c/b> (53 cells for seed 1): a perfect maze has exactly one path between any two cells. They differ in how much they explore (66 cells taken off the frontier for BFS against 133 for DFS on this seed).\u003c/li>\u003cli>With seeds 0 to 19 on 40 x 40 perfect mazes, both paths average about 1,070 cells. With loops the BFS path collapses (about 410 cells at 0.1 and 260 at 0.3) while the DFS path stays around 870: \u003cb>DFS finds a path, BFS finds the shortest\u003c/b>. DFS often takes fewer cells off its stack than BFS takes off its queue (about 2,100 against 2,700 at 0.1), which shows that exploring less is not the same as finding the best path.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The oracle test passes silently. If it fails, the usual cause is marking cells when they are popped, or a typo in the direction tuple.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The distance map counts up from both exits and the largest digits sit in dead ends far from both, where the two waves of the multi-source BFS meet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Each doubling of the side multiplies the open cells by 4 and the time by roughly 4 (ratios between 3.5 and 5.5 are normal noise): BFS is linear in the number of cells, a few seconds for the 1.3 million open cells at side 800 in pure Python.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Greedy best-first search usually takes far fewer cells than BFS on open mazes but can return a longer path; A*, in the shortest paths module, keeps the speed and restores optimality.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","l:graphs/how-do-you-find-the-fewest-moves-through-a-puzzl":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>When the \"position\" is a whole configuration (the digits on a lock, a word, the water in two jugs, a cell plus the keys you hold), make each configuration a \u003cb>vertex\u003c/b> and each legal move an \u003cb>edge\u003c/b>. BFS over that implicit graph gives the fewest moves. The state must capture \u003cb>everything that affects what you can do next\u003c/b>, must be \u003cb>hashable\u003c/b>, and the search costs about (number of states) x (moves per state), so count the states before you code.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You have a 3-litre jug, a 5-litre jug and a tap, and you need exactly 4 litres. Each moment is fully described by two numbers, the water in each jug: (0, 0) at the start. A move (fill, empty or pour one jug into the other) takes you to another pair. There are at most 4 x 6 = 24 pairs, so \"fewest moves to get 4 litres\" is BFS on a graph of 24 vertices that you never draw.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Define the state.\u003c/b> Ask: if I froze the puzzle now, what must I write down to resume it? Jugs: (a, b). Lock: the 4-digit string. Word ladder: the current word. Grid with keys: (row, column, keys held). Grid where you may break k walls: (row, column, breaks left).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Encode it hashably.\u003c/b> Tuples, strings, integers and frozensets work as dict keys; lists and sets do not. Bitmasks are compact for \"which of up to 20 items\" (\u003ccode>keys | 1 &lt;&lt; i\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Generate neighbours on demand\u003c/b>: a function from a state to the states one move away. Never build the whole graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>BFS with a visited dict\u003c/b> keyed by the full state. Record parents if you need the moves, not just their number.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forbidden states\u003c/b> (dead ends, walls) are simplest to pre-mark as visited, so they are never enqueued. Check whether the start itself is forbidden.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch5>Word ladders: neighbours by pattern\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Comparing a word with every other word to find one-letter neighbours costs O(N L) per word. Instead, give every word its L wildcard patterns (\"hot\" -&gt; \u003ccode>*ot\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>h*t\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ho*\u003c/code>) and group words by pattern. Two words are neighbours exactly when they share a pattern, so the neighbours of a word come from L bucket lookups. Building the buckets costs O(N L^2) (N words, L patterns each, L characters per pattern).\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Searching from both ends\u003c/h5>\u003cp>If every state has b neighbours and the answer is d moves away, BFS from the start touches about b^d states. Growing one search from the start and one from the goal, always expanding the smaller frontier, and stopping when they meet touches about 2 b^(d/2). For word ladders and similar puzzles that is often the difference between seconds and milliseconds.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Time is O(S x b x c): S reachable states, b moves from each, and c to build and hash one state. For the lock, S = 10^4, b = 8 and c is a 4-character string, so about 80,000 cheap steps. Memory is O(S) for the visited dict. Before coding, multiply out the state components: (row, column, breaks left) on a 40 x 40 grid with up to 1,600 breaks is 2.6 million states, which tells you to look for a cap (more than rows + columns breaks never help).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Question\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fewest moves, each move costing the same\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS over states\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Moves with different costs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dijkstra over states (shortest paths module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Count the ways, or optimise, when states never repeat along a path\u003c/td>\u003ctd>dynamic programming over states\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>List every solution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>backtracking\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Huge space and a good estimate of the remaining distance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A* (shortest paths module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Cues: \"minimum number of operations, moves, steps, transformations\"; small configurations (4 digits, a 2 x 3 board, words of length 10); a twist that changes what you can do later (keys, remaining breaks, direction), which must go into the state.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Jugs of 3 and 5 litres, target 4 litres in either jug. Each step is one BFS level; states are (small jug, big jug).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 0: both jugs empty\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frontier  \u003cmark>(0,0)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 1: fill the small jug, or fill the big jug\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frontier  \u003cmark>(3,0)\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>(0,5)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 2: new states only; emptying a jug leads back to (0,0), which is already visited\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frontier  \u003cmark>(3,5)\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>(0,3)\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>(3,2)\u003c/mark>\nseen      (0,0) (3,0) (0,5)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frontier  \u003cmark>(3,3)\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>(0,2)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frontier  \u003cmark>(1,5)\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>(2,0)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frontier  \u003cmark>(1,0)\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>(2,5)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Level 6: (3,4) has 4 litres in the big jug. Six moves is the minimum\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frontier  (0,1)  \u003cmark>(3,4)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Follow parents back from (3,4): fill big, pour into small, empty small, pour, fill big, pour\u003c/p>\u003cpre>path      (0,0) -&gt; (0,5) -&gt; (3,2) -&gt; (0,2) -&gt; (2,0) -&gt; (2,5) -&gt; \u003cmark>(3,4)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque, defaultdict\n\ndef bfs_path(start, is_goal, neighbours):\n    \"\"\"Fewest-move list of states from start to a goal state, or None. States must be hashable.\"\"\"\n    parent = {start: None}\n    queue = deque([start])\n    while queue:\n        state = queue.popleft()\n        if is_goal(state):\n            path = []\n            while state is not None:\n                path.append(state)\n                state = parent[state]\n            return path[::-1]\n        for nxt in neighbours(state):\n            if nxt not in parent:          # visited is keyed by the whole state\n                parent[nxt] = state\n                queue.append(nxt)\n    return None\n\n\ndef jug_moves(state, cap_a=3, cap_b=5):\n    \"\"\"States one move from (a, b): fill, empty, or pour one jug into the other.\"\"\"\n    a, b = state\n    ab = min(a, cap_b - b)                 # how much can go from a into b\n    ba = min(b, cap_a - a)\n    return [(cap_a, b), (a, cap_b), (0, b), (a, 0), (a - ab, b + ab), (a + ba, b - ba)]\n\n\ndef pattern_buckets(words):\n    \"\"\"Wildcard pattern ('h*t') -&gt; words matching it; neighbours share a pattern.\"\"\"\n    buckets = defaultdict(list)\n    for w in words:\n        for i in range(len(w)):\n            buckets[w[:i] + '*' + w[i + 1:]].append(w)\n    return buckets\n\n\npath = bfs_path((0, 0), lambda s: 4 in s, jug_moves)   # 7 states, 6 moves\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Pre-mark forbidden states by putting them in \u003ccode>parent\u003c/code> before the search starts (and return early if the start is forbidden).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Visited by position only\u003c/b> when the state has more parts: a cell reached with no keys is not the same state as that cell reached with a key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unhashable states\u003c/b>: lists cannot be dict keys; convert to tuples or strings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forbidden start\u003c/b>: if the start state is a dead end, the answer is \"impossible\", not 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting levels by hand\u003c/b>: store the distance (or the parent) with each state instead of a separate counter that drifts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>State explosion\u003c/b>: estimate S before coding; if it is in the hundreds of millions, look for a cap, a symmetry, or a smarter model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Rebuilding expensive neighbour lists\u003c/b>: precompute buckets or move tables once, outside the search.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the jug trace, why do only three states appear at level 2 when two states each have six moves?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Many moves lead to states already seen. From (3,0): filling the small jug gives (3,0) itself, emptying it gives (0,0), emptying the big jug gives (3,0) again; only (3,5) and (0,3) (pour small into big) are new. From (0,5): (3,5) is already labelled this level, (0,0) is old, and pouring gives (3,2). So level 2 is (3,5), (0,3), (3,2).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In a grid where you must collect keys to open doors, why is (row, column) not a valid state?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whether you can pass a door depends on the keys you hold, so two visits to the same cell with different keys have different futures. Keyed only by position, the first visit (perhaps without a key) blocks a later, more useful visit with the key. The state must be (row, column, keys held), for example with keys as a bitmask.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A grid problem allows breaking up to k walls, with a 40 x 40 grid and k up to 1,600. How many states, and how do you cut them down?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(row, column, breaks left) gives 40 x 40 x 1,601, about 2.6 million. Any shortest path needs at most rows + columns - 2 = 78 steps, so with k at least 78 you can walk the straight Manhattan route and return 78 at once; otherwise k is at most 77 and there are at most 40 x 40 x 78 = 124,800 states.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A lock solver puts dead ends in the visited set but returns 0 immediately when the target is \"0000\". With dead ends \u003ccode>[\"0000\"]\u003c/code> and target \"0000\", what should it return, and what does it return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It should return -1: the lock starts on a dead end and cannot even begin. It returns 0 because the target check happens before the dead-end check. Test the start against the forbidden set first.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>752 Open the Lock.\u003c/b> States are the 10,000 four-digit strings; each has 8 neighbours (one wheel up or down, with 9 and 0 wrapping). Pre-mark dead ends, and handle a dead start. Expected: O(10^4 x 8) time; target \"0009\" with no dead ends takes 1 turn; \"5555\" takes 20; if all eight neighbours of \"0000\" are dead ends, target \"0011\" gives -1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>127 Word Ladder (stretch).\u003c/b> BFS over words with pattern buckets; the answer counts words, not moves. Expected: O(N L^2); from \"cold\" to \"warm\" with the list \u003ccode>[\"cord\",\"card\",\"ward\",\"warm\",\"worm\",\"word\",\"wore\",\"core\",\"corm\"]\u003c/code> the answer is 5 (cold, cord, card, ward, warm); if \"warm\" is not in the list, 0.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to turn a puzzle into a graph of states, choose what a state must contain, and search it with BFS within a predictable budget.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/how-do-you-detect-a-cycle-and-when-can-a-graph-b":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In a \u003cb>directed\u003c/b> graph, run DFS with three colours: white (unseen), grey (on the current path) and black (finished). An edge into a \u003cb>grey\u003c/b> vertex returns to your own path, so it closes a cycle; an edge into a black vertex does not. In an \u003cb>undirected\u003c/b> graph, meeting a visited vertex that is not the one you came from closes a cycle. A graph can be \u003cb>two-coloured\u003c/b> (every edge joins different colours) exactly when it has no odd cycle, and BFS that alternates colours finds out.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Walk through a building unrolling a ball of string behind you, and roll it back up when you retreat from a dead end. The string marks your current path (grey). Rooms you have fully explored and left are black. If a one-way door leads you into a room your string already passes through, you have walked in a circle. If it leads into a black room, you have simply reached territory explored earlier from somewhere else, and nothing in there leads back to you (otherwise you would have found it then).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Directed graphs: three colours\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>visit(u)\u003c/code>: colour u grey; for each edge u -&gt; v, if v is grey report a cycle, if v is white visit it; when all edges are done colour u black. A grey vertex is an ancestor of u in the DFS (it is on the call stack), so u -&gt; v plus the tree path v ... u is a cycle. Conversely, if a cycle exists, take its first vertex x that DFS colours grey: every other cycle vertex is still white and reachable from x, so they all turn grey while x is grey, and the edge back into x is examined then, finding x grey.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why one \"visited\" flag is wrong\u003c/b>: in the graph A -&gt; B, A -&gt; C, C -&gt; B, DFS finishes B before reaching it again from C. A single flag says \"seen B before: cycle!\", but there is none. Black means \"finished, cannot lead back to you\".\u003c/p>\u003cp>To report the cycle, store each vertex's DFS parent. When the edge u -&gt; v hits grey v, walk parents from u back to v.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Undirected graphs: the parent rule\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Every undirected edge is seen from both ends, so the vertex you just came from always looks visited. Ignore it; any \u003cb>other\u003c/b> visited neighbour means a cycle. (With parallel edges between the same two vertices, compare edge ids instead of vertices.) A cheaper test when you only need yes or no: a forest has exactly V - C edges, where C is the number of components; any more edges and a cycle exists. Union-find, in the next module, detects the first edge that closes a cycle as edges arrive.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Two-colouring (bipartite graphs)\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Colour a start vertex 0 and give each newly discovered neighbour the opposite colour of the vertex that found it. If an edge ever joins two vertices of the same colour, the graph is not bipartite: following the BFS tree from both ends back to their common ancestor and adding that edge gives a cycle of odd length, and an odd cycle cannot alternate two colours. If no conflict appears, the colouring is a valid split. Run it from every uncoloured vertex, because the graph may be disconnected.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each algorithm is a DFS or BFS: \u003cb>O(V + E) time, O(V) space\u003c/b>. The recursive three-colour DFS can recurse V deep, so use the iterative version (below) for large graphs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\"Is it possible to finish all tasks / courses?\" (a directed cycle makes it impossible), \"find the circular dependency\", deadlock detection in a waits-for graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\"Can these people be split into two groups with no conflict inside a group?\", \"can the map be coloured with two colours?\", \"two shifts with no clash\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>Undirected \"is this edge list a tree?\" (connected and exactly V - 1 edges, or no cycle and connected).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Not for \"how many cycles\" or \"longest cycle\" in general graphs: those are much harder (only special shapes, such as every vertex having one outgoing edge, are easy).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Graph\u003c/th>\u003cth>Has a cycle?\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Directed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>edge into a grey vertex\u003c/td>\u003ctd>three-colour DFS, or Kahn leaves vertices behind\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Undirected\u003c/td>\u003ctd>visited neighbour that is not the parent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DFS or BFS with parents, or union-find\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Any, two groups\u003c/td>\u003ctd>edge inside a colour class (odd cycle)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS two-colouring\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Directed graph A -&gt; B, A -&gt; C, C -&gt; B, C -&gt; D, D -&gt; E, E -&gt; C. DFS from A.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Visit A: it turns grey (on the current path)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A:\u003cmark>grey\u003c/mark>  B:white  C:white  D:white  E:white\npath  [A]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">A -&gt; B: B is white, so visit it. B has no edges and finishes: black\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A:grey  B:\u003cmark>black\u003c/mark>  C:white  D:white  E:white\npath  [A]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">A -&gt; C: C is white, visit it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A:grey  B:black  C:\u003cmark>grey\u003c/mark>  D:white  E:white\npath  [A, C]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">C -&gt; B: B is black, already finished. Not a cycle (a single visited flag would wrongly say yes)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A:grey  \u003cmark>B:black\u003c/mark>  C:grey  D:white  E:white\npath  [A, C]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">C -&gt; D and D -&gt; E: both white, both turn grey\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A:grey  B:black  C:grey  D:\u003cmark>grey\u003c/mark>  E:\u003cmark>grey\u003c/mark>\npath  [A, C, D, E]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">E -&gt; C: C is grey, it is on the current path. Back edge: the cycle is C -&gt; D -&gt; E -&gt; C\u003c/p>\u003cpre>A:grey  B:black  \u003cmark>C:grey\u003c/mark>  D:grey  E:grey\ncycle \u003cmark>C -&gt; D -&gt; E -&gt; C\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\nWHITE, GREY, BLACK = 0, 1, 2\n\ndef has_cycle(adj):\n    \"\"\"True if the directed graph on 0..n-1 has a cycle (recursive three-colour DFS).\"\"\"\n    colour = [WHITE] * len(adj)\n\n    def visit(u):\n        colour[u] = GREY                    # u is on the current path\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            if colour[v] == GREY:           # back edge: v is an ancestor of u\n                return True\n            if colour[v] == WHITE and visit(v):\n                return True\n        colour[u] = BLACK                   # u and everything reachable from it are done\n        return False\n\n    return any(colour[s] == WHITE and visit(s) for s in range(len(adj)))\n\n\ndef find_cycle(adj):\n    \"\"\"One directed cycle as [v, ..., v], or None. Iterative, so deep graphs are fine.\"\"\"\n    colour = [WHITE] * len(adj)\n    parent = [-1] * len(adj)\n    for s in range(len(adj)):\n        if colour[s] != WHITE:\n            continue\n        colour[s] = GREY\n        stack = [(s, iter(adj[s]))]\n        while stack:\n            u, edges = stack[-1]\n            v = next(edges, None)\n            if v is None:                   # every edge of u explored\n                colour[u] = BLACK\n                stack.pop()\n            elif colour[v] == WHITE:\n                colour[v] = GREY\n                parent[v] = u\n                stack.append((v, iter(adj[v])))\n            elif colour[v] == GREY:         # u -&gt; v closes a cycle\n                cycle = [v]\n                while u != v:\n                    cycle.append(u)\n                    u = parent[u]\n                cycle.append(v)\n                return cycle[::-1]\n    return None\n\n\ndef two_colour(adj):\n    \"\"\"Colours 0/1 with every edge joining different colours, or None if there is an odd cycle.\"\"\"\n    colour = [-1] * len(adj)\n    for s in range(len(adj)):\n        if colour[s] != -1:\n            continue\n        colour[s] = 0\n        queue = deque([s])\n        while queue:\n            u = queue.popleft()\n            for v in adj[u]:\n                if colour[v] == -1:\n                    colour[v] = 1 - colour[u]\n                    queue.append(v)\n                elif colour[v] == colour[u]:\n                    return None\n    return colour\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>One visited flag in a directed graph\u003c/b> reports cycles that do not exist (any vertex reachable two ways).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting disconnected parts\u003c/b>: start from every white vertex, not just vertex 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Undirected graphs with the directed algorithm\u003c/b>: every edge looks like a back edge to the parent. Use the parent rule.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Parallel edges\u003c/b>: two edges between u and v form a cycle of length 2 that the vertex-based parent rule misses; compare edge ids.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deep recursion\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>has_cycle\u003c/code> recurses once per vertex on a long chain; use \u003ccode>find_cycle\u003c/code>'s iterator stack.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\u003ccode>next(edges, None)\u003c/code> with None as a real vertex\u003c/b>: fine for integers; use a unique sentinel object if None can be a vertex.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run three-colour DFS from A on A -&gt; B, B -&gt; C, A -&gt; C, C -&gt; D. Is there a cycle? Which edge would a single visited flag misreport?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No cycle. DFS goes A, B, C, D; D, C and B finish black; then A -&gt; C finds C black. A single visited flag would call A -&gt; C a cycle.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does an edge into a black vertex never close a cycle?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A vertex turns black only after DFS has explored everything reachable from it. If a black v could reach the current vertex u, then u (white at that time) would have been reached and finished inside v's search, so u would be black too, not grey on the current path. So no path leads from v back to u, and u -&gt; v cannot close a cycle.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Twelve students must be split into two teams so that no pair who dislike each other share a team. You have the list of dislikes. What do you run, and what proves it impossible?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>BFS (or DFS) two-colouring over the dislike graph, started from every uncoloured student. It is impossible exactly when some dislike edge joins two students of the same colour, which means an odd cycle of dislikes (A dislikes B, B dislikes C, C dislikes A) exists.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Someone checks an undirected graph for cycles with the three-colour DFS and finds a \"cycle\" in a simple path 0 - 1 - 2. Why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The undirected edge 0 - 1 is stored in both lists. While 1 is grey, DFS from 1 looks at its neighbour 0, which is grey (an ancestor), and reports a back edge. Undirected graphs need the parent rule: skip the vertex you came from.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>785 Is Graph Bipartite?\u003c/b> Two-colour from every uncoloured vertex (the graph may be disconnected). Expected: O(V + E); the 4-cycle \u003ccode>[[1,3],[0,2],[1,3],[0,2]]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>true\u003c/code>; the triangle \u003ccode>[[1,2],[0,2],[0,1]]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>false\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>210 Course Schedule II, cycle half.\u003c/b> Solve it with three-colour DFS: a back edge means return \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>; otherwise output vertices in reverse finishing order (the next card explains why that order is valid). Expected: O(V + E); n = 2 with \u003ccode>[[0,1],[1,0]]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find directed and undirected cycles, recover the cycle itself, and decide whether a graph splits into two conflict-free sides.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/how-do-you-order-tasks-that-depend-on-each-other":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>topological order\u003c/b> lists the vertices of a directed graph so that every edge u -&gt; v has u before v. One exists exactly when the graph has \u003cb>no directed cycle\u003c/b> (it is a DAG). \u003cb>Kahn's algorithm\u003c/b> repeatedly outputs a vertex with no remaining incoming edges and deletes its outgoing edges; if it outputs fewer than V vertices, the rest are on a cycle or downstream of one. Alternatively, \u003cb>reverse the DFS finishing order\u003c/b>. Both run in O(V + E).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Getting dressed: socks before shoes, shirt before tie, trousers before belt. At any moment you can put on anything whose prerequisites are already on. Keep doing that and you get a valid order. If socks needed shoes and shoes needed socks, you would get stuck with both still in the drawer: that is a cycle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Kahn's algorithm\u003c/h5>\u003col>\u003cli>Compute every vertex's \u003cb>in-degree\u003c/b> (number of incoming edges).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Put all vertices with in-degree 0 in a \"ready\" queue: nothing needs to happen before them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pop a vertex, append it to the order, and decrement the in-degree of each of its successors; any that reach 0 become ready.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the order has V vertices, it is a topological order. Otherwise no order exists.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>\u003cb>Why the order is valid\u003c/b>: a vertex is output only when its in-degree has reached 0, which happens only after every predecessor has been output and has decremented it. \u003cb>Why a cycle stops it\u003c/b>: on a cycle, every vertex has a predecessor on the same cycle that must be output first, so none of them can ever be the first to reach in-degree 0. Vertices downstream of a cycle are stuck too, because their in-degree still counts an edge from a stuck vertex.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>DFS finishing order\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Run DFS (three colours) from every white vertex and record each vertex when it \u003cb>finishes\u003c/b>. Reverse that list. For any edge u -&gt; v explored while u is grey: if v is white, it is visited and finishes inside u's call, so before u; if v is black, it already finished; v cannot be grey, because that would be a cycle. So every edge goes from a later-finishing vertex to an earlier one, and the reversed finishing order puts u before v. A grey hit doubles as cycle detection.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Choosing and shaping the order\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Many valid orders\u003c/b> usually exist. To get the lexicographically smallest, replace the queue with a min-heap (\u003ccode>heapq\u003c/code>). The order is \u003cb>unique\u003c/b> exactly when the ready set never holds more than one vertex.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>graphlib\u003c/b> (standard library since Python 3.9) maps each node to its \u003cb>predecessors\u003c/b>, the opposite of an adjacency list: \u003ccode>{\"app\": [\"lib\"]}\u003c/code> means lib comes first. \u003ccode>static_order()\u003c/code> yields a valid order or raises \u003ccode>CycleError\u003c/code> whose second argument lists a cycle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>DP on a DAG\u003c/b>: once you have a topological order, any quantity that flows along edges (longest path, number of paths, earliest finish time) can be computed in one pass in that order. The next card does exactly that for build schedules.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Computing in-degrees scans every edge once: O(V + E). Each vertex enters and leaves the ready queue once, and each edge is decremented once: O(V + E) in total, O(V) extra space. With a heap for the smallest order, each push and pop costs O(log V): O(V log V + E). The DFS version is O(V + E) as well.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\"Prerequisites\", \"depends on\", \"must happen before\", \"build order\", \"install order\", \"is it possible to finish all\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>An alphabet order recovered from a sorted word list (compare adjacent words to get edges, then sort topologically).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Any DP whose subproblems form a DAG that is not a simple line or grid.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Prefer \u003cb>Kahn\u003c/b> when you want waves of tasks that can run together, a deterministic smallest order, or no recursion. Prefer \u003cb>DFS\u003c/b> when you also need the cycle itself to report it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Not for undirected graphs (there is no \"before\"), and not when cycles are legal and must be collapsed first (strongly connected components, outlined in the shortest paths module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Kahn's algorithm on seven tasks where e and f depend on each other by mistake:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>    a ---&gt; b ---&gt; d\n    |             ^\n    +----&gt; c -----+\n           |\n           v\n           e ---&gt; f ---&gt; g\n           ^      |\n           +------+\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">In-degrees: only a has none, so the ready queue is [a]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>vertex   a  b  c  d  e  f  g\nindeg    0  1  1  2  2  1  1\nready    [\u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>]\norder    []\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop a: b and c drop to 0 and become ready\u003c/p>\u003cpre>indeg    0  \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>  2  2  1  1\nready    [b, c]\norder    [a]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop b: d drops to 1, not yet ready (it still waits for c)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>indeg    0  0  0  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  2  1  1\nready    [c]\norder    [a, b]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop c: d drops to 0 and is ready; e drops to 1 (it still waits for f)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>indeg    0  0  0  \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  1  1\nready    [d]\norder    [a, b, c]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop d: it has no outgoing edges. The ready queue is now empty\u003c/p>\u003cpre>indeg    0  0  0  0  1  1  1\nready    []\norder    [a, b, c, d]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Stuck with 4 of 7 output: e and f wait for each other (a cycle), g waits for f (downstream of it)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>indeg    0  0  0  0  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nleft     \u003cmark>e  f  g\u003c/mark>   so there is no topological order\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef topo_kahn(n, edges):\n    \"\"\"Order 0..n-1 so every edge (u, v) has u before v; None if there is a cycle.\"\"\"\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    indeg = [0] * n\n    for u, v in edges:\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        indeg[v] += 1\n    ready = deque(v for v in range(n) if indeg[v] == 0)\n    order = []\n    while ready:\n        u = ready.popleft()\n        order.append(u)\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            indeg[v] -= 1\n            if indeg[v] == 0:\n                ready.append(v)\n    return order if len(order) == n else None\n\n\ndef topo_dfs(n, edges):\n    \"\"\"Reverse DFS finishing order (iterative); None if there is a cycle.\"\"\"\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for u, v in edges:\n        adj[u].append(v)\n    colour = [0] * n                        # 0 white, 1 grey, 2 black\n    finished = []\n    for s in range(n):\n        if colour[s]:\n            continue\n        colour[s] = 1\n        stack = [(s, iter(adj[s]))]\n        while stack:\n            u, it = stack[-1]\n            v = next(it, None)\n            if v is None:\n                colour[u] = 2\n                finished.append(u)\n                stack.pop()\n            elif colour[v] == 1:\n                return None                 # back edge: cycle\n            elif colour[v] == 0:\n                colour[v] = 1\n                stack.append((v, iter(adj[v])))\n    return finished[::-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With the standard library:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from graphlib import TopologicalSorter, CycleError\n\ndeps = {\"app\": [\"lib\", \"config\"], \"lib\": [\"utils\"], \"config\": [], \"utils\": []}   # node -&gt; predecessors\nprint(list(TopologicalSorter(deps).static_order()))    # ['config', 'utils', 'lib', 'app']\n\ntry:\n    list(TopologicalSorter({\"a\": [\"b\"], \"b\": [\"c\"], \"c\": [\"a\"]}).static_order())\nexcept CycleError as err:\n    print(err.args[1])                                  # ['a', 'c', 'b', 'a']\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Edge direction\u003c/b>: from the prerequisite to the dependent. In graphlib it is the other way round: a node maps to its predecessors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Returning a partial order\u003c/b> when a cycle exists: always compare the length with V.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Isolated vertices\u003c/b>: build in-degrees for all n vertices, not just those that appear in edges, or they never get output.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Duplicate edges\u003c/b> are fine in Kahn as long as each copy is both counted and decremented; do not dedupe one side only.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recursion\u003c/b> in the DFS version on long dependency chains; use the iterative form.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expecting one particular order\u003c/b> when the problem accepts any valid one; check validity, not equality, in your tests.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Remove the edge f -&gt; e from the trace. What order does Kahn's algorithm (FIFO queue) produce?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>a, b, c, d, e, f, g. After c is popped, both d and e reach in-degree 0 (e now waits only for c), so the queue holds [d, e]; then e releases f and f releases g.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does g end up left behind even though it is not on a cycle?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Its in-degree counts the edge f -&gt; g. That edge is removed only when f is output, and f never is, because it waits for e, which waits for f. Kahn's leftovers are the cycle vertices plus everything reachable from them.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You must report \"cannot build: gen -&gt; lib -&gt; gen\" rather than just \"there is a cycle\". Kahn, DFS, or both?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Kahn tells you a cycle exists and which vertices are stuck, but not the cycle path. Run three-colour DFS (on the stuck vertices, or the whole graph) and walk parents back from the back edge to print the cycle. Build tools typically do both: Kahn for the order, DFS for the error message.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague passes \u003ccode>{\"lib\": [\"app\"]}\u003c/code> to TopologicalSorter, meaning \"lib must be built before app\". What order comes out, and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>['app', 'lib']\u003c/code>: graphlib reads the dict as \"lib's predecessors are [app]\", so app comes first. Write \u003ccode>{\"app\": [\"lib\"]}\u003c/code>: each node maps to the things that must come before it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>210 Course Schedule II.\u003c/b> Kahn's algorithm with edges prerequisite -&gt; course; return \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code> if fewer than n courses come out. Expected: O(V + E); n = 4 with \u003ccode>[[1,0],[2,0],[3,1],[3,2]]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>[0,1,2,3]\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>[0,2,1,3]\u003c/code>, also valid); n = 2 with \u003ccode>[[0,1],[1,0]]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>802 Find Eventual Safe States (stretch).\u003c/b> A node is safe when every path from it ends at a node with no outgoing edges. Reverse the edges and run Kahn from the terminal nodes using out-degrees: whatever gets output is safe. Expected: O(V + E); \u003ccode>[[1],[2],[0,3],[]]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>[3]\u003c/code> (0, 1 and 2 form a cycle); \u003ccode>[[1,2],[2,3],[5],[0],[5],[],[]]\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>[2,4,5,6]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to order a DAG with Kahn's algorithm or DFS finishing times, prove the order valid, and report when no order exists.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:graphs/how-do-build-tools-run-dependent-tasks-in-parall":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>With unlimited workers, a task can start as soon as its last prerequisite finishes, so \u003ccode>finish[t] = duration[t] + max(finish of t's prerequisites)\u003c/code>, computed in one pass over a \u003cb>topological order\u003c/b>. The largest finish time is the length of the \u003cb>critical path\u003c/b>, the longest chain of dependent work, and no number of workers can beat it. Real build tools run the \u003cb>ready-set loop\u003c/b>: start everything that is ready, and when a task finishes, release the tasks whose last prerequisite it was. Python's \u003ccode>graphlib\u003c/code> gives you that loop directly.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cooking dinner: rice takes 20 minutes, chopping takes 5 and frying the chopped vegetables takes 10, and the meal is served when both are done. With two cooks, rice and chopping start together; frying starts at minute 5 and ends at 15; dinner is at minute 20, set by the rice. Speeding up the chopping does nothing. Only work on the longest chain, the critical path, moves the finish time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Earliest finish times (longest path in a DAG)\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Process tasks in topological order, so every prerequisite is finished before you look at a task. A task starts at the latest finish among its prerequisites (0 if it has none) and finishes \u003ccode>duration\u003c/code> later. Remember which prerequisite finished last: following those links back from the task that finishes last spells out the critical path. This is dynamic programming over the DAG: the subproblems are tasks, and the topological order is the order to solve them in.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Lower bounds for P workers\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Two facts bound any schedule. It cannot finish before the critical path length L, since that chain runs one task at a time. And P workers do at most P units of work per unit of time, so it cannot finish before W / P, where W is the total work. The simple rule \"whenever a worker is free, give it any ready task\" (list scheduling) is never worse than (2 - 1/P) times the optimum, a classic result of Graham; preferring the ready task with the longest remaining chain usually does much better in practice.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The ready-set loop in real tools\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>make\u003c/b> brings a target's prerequisites up to date first, recursively, and rebuilds a target when it is older than a prerequisite; \u003ccode>make -j\u003c/code> runs independent recipes in parallel.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ninja\u003c/b> always builds in parallel, by default based on the number of CPUs, and its manual warns that underspecified dependencies give incorrect builds: if a step reads a file you did not declare, the scheduler is free to run it too early.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>graphlib\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>prepare()\u003c/code> checks for cycles (raising \u003ccode>CycleError\u003c/code>), \u003ccode>get_ready()\u003c/code> returns every task whose prerequisites are all done, and \u003ccode>done(task)\u003c/code> releases its dependents. \u003ccode>is_active()\u003c/code> stays true while anything is ready or running.\u003c/li>\u003cli>CI systems (for example jobs with \u003ccode>needs:\u003c/code> in GitHub Actions) and data pipelines (Airflow DAGs) are the same structure: tasks, dependencies, and a scheduler releasing ready work.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The earliest-finish pass visits every task once and every dependency once: \u003cb>O(V + E)\u003c/b> after (or during) the topological sort. The graphlib loop does O(V + E) bookkeeping in total plus the cost of the tasks themselves; with a thread pool, the waiting is in \u003ccode>wait()\u003c/code>, not in a busy loop.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\"Minimum time to finish all courses / jobs, with durations and unlimited parallelism\", \"earliest time each task can complete\", \"which tasks are critical\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build systems, CI pipelines, data pipelines and project plans (the critical path method in project management).\u003c/li>\u003cli>With limited workers the exact optimum is NP-hard in general; use list scheduling with the bounds above, or simulate with a heap of finish times.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Threads suit tasks that wait on I/O or subprocesses (compilers, tests); for CPU-bound Python functions use \u003ccode>ProcessPoolExecutor\u003c/code>, because of the GIL.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Six build tasks (durations in brackets): fetch (2); lint (1), compile (4) and docs (2) need fetch; test (3) needs compile; package (1) needs test, lint and docs. \u003ccode>static_order()\u003c/code> gives fetch, lint, compile, docs, test, package.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">fetch has no prerequisites: start 0, finish 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>task     fetch  lint  compile  docs  test  package\nfinish   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">lint, compile and docs each need only fetch: they all start at 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>finish   2  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">test needs compile: start 6, finish 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>finish   2  3  6  4  \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">package needs test (9), lint (3) and docs (4): start at the latest, 9; finish 10\u003c/p>\u003cpre>finish   2  3  6  4  9  \u003cmark>10\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Walk back along the prerequisite that finished last: the critical path has length 10\u003c/p>\u003cpre>path     \u003cmark>fetch -&gt; compile -&gt; test -&gt; package\u003c/mark>   (2 + 4 + 3 + 1)\nbounds   total work 13; with 2 workers no schedule beats max(10, 13 / 2) = 10\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">graphlib's waves (call done on each whole batch): independent tasks are released together\u003c/p>\u003cpre>get_ready  ('fetch',)  ('lint', 'compile', 'docs')  ('test',)  ('package',)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, wait, FIRST_COMPLETED\nfrom graphlib import TopologicalSorter\n\ndef critical_path(deps, duration):\n    \"\"\"Makespan with unlimited workers and one longest chain. deps maps task -&gt; prerequisites.\"\"\"\n    finish, last = {}, {}\n    for task in TopologicalSorter(deps).static_order():\n        before = deps.get(task, ())\n        last[task] = max(before, key=finish.__getitem__, default=None)  # latest prerequisite\n        finish[task] = duration[task] + (finish[last[task]] if last[task] is not None else 0)\n    task = max(finish, key=finish.__getitem__)\n    makespan, chain = finish[task], []\n    while task is not None:\n        chain.append(task)\n        task = last[task]\n    return makespan, chain[::-1]\n\n\ndef run_all(deps, run, workers=4):\n    \"\"\"Call run(task) for every task, each after its prerequisites, up to `workers` at a time.\"\"\"\n    sorter = TopologicalSorter(deps)\n    sorter.prepare()                        # raises graphlib.CycleError on a cycle\n    with ThreadPoolExecutor(workers) as pool:\n        running = {}\n        while sorter.is_active():\n            for task in sorter.get_ready():\n                running[pool.submit(run, task)] = task\n            finished, _ = wait(running, return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED)\n            for future in finished:\n                future.result()             # re-raise a task's exception\n                sorter.done(running.pop(future))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting \u003ccode>done(task)\u003c/code>\u003c/b>: its dependents are never released and the run stops short or waits forever.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Swallowed failures\u003c/b>: if you never call \u003ccode>future.result()\u003c/code>, a failed task looks finished and its dependents run on missing outputs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Undeclared dependencies\u003c/b>: a task that reads another's output without saying so works sequentially and breaks in parallel, intermittently.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Wrong graph format\u003c/b>: graphlib wants node -&gt; predecessors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Threads for CPU-bound Python\u003c/b>: the GIL serialises them; use processes or subprocesses.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Minimising time with limited workers by brute force\u003c/b>: the problem is NP-hard; use the bounds and a good heuristic.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the trace, compile now takes 1 instead of 4. What is the makespan with unlimited workers, and which chain is critical?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>compile finishes at 3, test at 6, docs at 4, lint at 3, so package starts at 6 and finishes at 7. The critical path is still fetch -&gt; compile -&gt; test -&gt; package, now 2 + 1 + 3 + 1 = 7.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can no schedule on P workers finish before W / P, where W is the total work?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In a schedule of length T, each worker does at most T units of work, so all P together do at most P x T. All W units must be done, so P x T &gt;= W, that is T &gt;= W / P.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Course durations are given and any number of courses can run at once, but a course starts only after all its prerequisites end. How do you get the minimum total time, and in what time complexity?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Topological order, then \u003ccode>finish[c] = time[c] + max(finish of c's prerequisites)\u003c/code> (0 if none); the answer is the largest finish. O(V + E), and Kahn's algorithm can compute it as it goes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague's loop calls \u003ccode>sorter.get_ready()\u003c/code> once, submits those tasks, waits for all of them, and stops. What is wrong?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Only the first wave runs. Dependents become ready only after \u003ccode>done()\u003c/code> is called for their prerequisites, so the loop must keep calling \u003ccode>get_ready()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>done()\u003c/code> while \u003ccode>is_active()\u003c/code> is true, as in the template.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>2050 Parallel Courses III (stretch).\u003c/b> Earliest finish times in topological order; the answer is the largest. Expected: O(n + E); n = 4, relations \u003ccode>[[1,2],[1,3],[2,4],[3,4]]\u003c/code>, times \u003ccode>[2,3,1,4]\u003c/code> gives 9 (courses 1, 2, 4: 2 + 3 + 4).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Your own runner.\u003c/b> Run \u003ccode>run_all\u003c/code> on the six build tasks from the trace with \u003ccode>run\u003c/code> sleeping \u003ccode>duration / 10\u003c/code> seconds and printing start and end times, first with \u003ccode>workers=1\u003c/code>, then 2, then 4. Expected: about 1.3 s with one worker (the total work), and about 1.0 s with two or more workers (the critical path); the start times never violate a dependency.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to compute earliest finish times and the critical path of a DAG, bound any schedule, and run dependent tasks in parallel with graphlib.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","p:1971":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> vertices labelled from zero, and each pair in \u003ccode>edges\u003c/code> describes a connection usable in either direction. Determine whether \u003ccode>destination\u003c/code> can be reached from \u003ccode>source\u003c/code> by following zero or more connections. For example, edges \u003ccode>[[0, 1], [1, 2]]\u003c/code> connect 0 to 2 through 1.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Labels are in \u003ccode>0..n-1\u003c/code>; there may be many vertices and edges, so avoid looking through the complete edge list for every step. A vertex is reachable from itself without traversing an edge. Connections need not make the entire graph connected.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Computers joined by cables given as a list of pairs; can one computer reach another?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An edge list cannot be walked directly. What structure lets you list a vertex's neighbours quickly, and what do you run on it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Modelling a problem as a graph\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build undirected adjacency lists (each edge stored in both lists), then BFS or DFS from the source with a visited set; the answer is whether the destination gets marked. Every vertex and edge is handled once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(V + E) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Search from the source, but for each discovered vertex scan the whole edge list to find incident edges. With up to \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> discovered vertices this takes O(nE) time. A visited set is still necessary to stop repeated exploration around a cycle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Index the connections by endpoint once. Breadth-first search then expands each discovered vertex and marks neighbours immediately when enqueued. The queue contains only reachable vertices, and every reachable vertex is eventually expanded. Thus discovering the target proves a path exists; exhausting the queue proves none exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">edges = [[0, 1], [1, 2]], source = 0, destination = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue [0], seen {0}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Expand 0 then 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue [2], seen {0, 1, 2}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove 2, which is the destination\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef valid_path(n, edges, source, destination):\n    adjacency = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for u, v in edges:\n        adjacency[u].append(v)\n        adjacency[v].append(u)\n    queue = deque([source])\n    seen = {source}\n    while queue:\n        u = queue.popleft()\n        if u == destination:\n            return True\n        for v in adjacency[u]:\n            if v not in seen:\n                seen.add(v)\n                queue.append(v)\n    return False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building and traversing the adjacency lists takes O(V + E) time and O(V + E) space, including the graph representation. The search alone needs O(V) additional space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Add both orientations; the pairs describe undirected connections.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mark at enqueue time, not after expansion, to avoid duplicate queue entries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An isolated source equals the target only when both labels are the same.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return an actual path by recording each newly discovered vertex's predecessor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Answer many queries using connected-component labels computed once.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:733":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a rectangular image, a starting row and column, and a replacement colour, recolour exactly the pixels connected to the start through horizontal or vertical steps whose original colour equals the starting colour. Modify and return the image. Diagonal contact alone does not connect pixels.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The image is nonempty and the start is in bounds. A whole image can be one region, so recursion depth could be proportional to its area. If the replacement equals the starting colour, changing a pixel cannot serve as a visited mark; return immediately in that case.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Recolour a pixel and every same-coloured pixel connected to it sideways\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which pixels change? Those you can reach from the start by stepping only onto the old colour. What stops you from visiting a pixel twice?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> DFS and connected components\u003c/p>\u003cp>DFS or BFS from the start, stepping to 4-neighbours that still have the old colour and recolouring as you push, so the new colour is the visited mark. If the new colour equals the old one, return at once; otherwise the fill never ends.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m n) time, O(m n) space for the stack in the worst case\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Repeatedly scan every pixel, adding a pixel to a set when an already selected neighbour has the starting colour, until a pass makes no changes. A long winding region can require O(mn) passes over O(mn) pixels, giving O((mn)²) time and O(mn) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use an explicit stack for a graph search. Change the start before pushing it, and change every eligible neighbour before pushing it too. Since the new colour differs from the old colour, a changed pixel cannot be added twice. The search reaches precisely the old-colour component: each added pixel has an eligible path from the start, and every eligible neighbour of every reached pixel is eventually considered.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">image = [[1, 1], [1, 0]], start = (0, 0), replacement = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>recolour and push (0, 0)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The fill reaches (0, 1) and (1, 0), but not the 0 pixel\u003c/p>\u003cpre>image \u003cmark>[[2, 2], [2, 0]]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def flood_fill(image, sr, sc, color):\n    rows, cols = len(image), len(image[0])\n    old = image[sr][sc]\n    if old == color:\n        return image\n    image[sr][sc] = color\n    stack = [(sr, sc)]\n    while stack:\n        r, c = stack.pop()\n        for dr, dc in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 &lt;= nr &lt; rows and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; cols and image[nr][nc] == old:\n                image[nr][nc] = color\n                stack.append((nr, nc))\n    return image\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each pixel enters the stack at most once and checks four neighbours: O(mn) time and O(mn) worst-case auxiliary space. The input image is modified in place.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Returning early for an unchanged colour prevents infinite revisits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check row and column bounds separately; the image need not be square.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pixels with the same colour in a separate component remain unchanged.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Preserve the original image by copying it before the fill.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Fill eight-connected rather than four-connected regions by adding diagonal directions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:200":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the separate land masses in a rectangular grid of \u003ccode>\"1\"\u003c/code> land and \u003ccode>\"0\"\u003c/code> water. Land cells belong to the same mass if a chain of horizontal or vertical land neighbours joins them. For \u003ccode>[['1', '0'], ['0', '1']]\u003c/code>, the answer is two: corner contact does not join the cells.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The grid is nonempty and rectangular. It may contain only water or only land, and one connected region can cover every cell. Each cell has a constant number of potential neighbours, so a traversal over the implicit graph is linear in the grid area. The implementation below changes visited land to water; copy the rows first if the caller needs to preserve them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Mutating the grid changes subsequent calls on the same object: after the first call, all original land has been erased. The number of original water cells is irrelevant to the count except that they separate components. Boundary checks ensure that cells at opposite ends of a row never wrap around.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count groups of '1' cells that touch sideways\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If you start a fill at a land cell nobody has visited yet, how much of the grid does that one fill account for?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> DFS and connected components\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan every cell; each unvisited land cell starts a flood fill (iterative DFS or BFS) that marks its whole island, and adds one to the count. Every cell is marked once, so the scan and all the fills together are linear.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m n) time, O(m n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each land cell, run a new search to construct its entire reachable set, then deduplicate these sets. If all \u003ccode>mn\u003c/code> cells are land, this repeats a linear-size traversal for each cell: O((mn)²) time. Storing all sets can also require quadratic space. The redundancy is searching the same component from every one of its members.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Scan cells in any fixed order. When encountering unmarked land, increment the count and flood its entire component, marking cells as soon as they are pushed. The next unmarked land cannot be in any previously counted component: a complete flood would have marked it. Conversely, every land component contains a first cell in the scan, so each component contributes exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">grid = [['1', '1', '0'], ['0', '1', '1'], ['1', '0', '0']]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first land at (0, 0), count 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Flood reaches (0, 1), (1, 1), and (1, 2)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>all four become '0'\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Scan reaches (2, 0), which starts a second flood\u003c/p>\u003cpre>count \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def num_islands(grid):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    islands = 0\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != \"1\":\n                continue\n            islands += 1\n            grid[r][c] = \"0\"\n            stack = [(r, c)]\n            while stack:\n                x, y = stack.pop()\n                for dx, dy in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n                    nx, ny = x + dx, y + dy\n                    if 0 &lt;= nx &lt; rows and 0 &lt;= ny &lt; cols and grid[nx][ny] == \"1\":\n                        grid[nx][ny] = \"0\"\n                        stack.append((nx, ny))\n    return islands\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The outer scan touches every cell once; all floods together push each land cell once and inspect four neighbours. Total time is O(mn), not O((mn)²). The stack can hold O(mn) cells; the algorithm uses the input itself for marks.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compare strings, not integer \u003ccode>1\u003c/code>; the grid stores character values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mark before pushing, otherwise multiple neighbours can push the same land cell.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Water-only grids return zero; diagonal-only contacts remain separate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Iteration avoids a recursive call stack overflow on a large single island.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Record each flood's size to find the largest island.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Leave the grid unchanged by maintaining a separate set of visited coordinates.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count islands under eight-directional connectivity instead.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1091":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In a square binary grid, find the minimum number of cells in a route from the upper-left cell to the lower-right cell. A route may use any of eight neighbouring directions but may visit only zero-valued cells. Return \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code> if no route exists. For a single open cell, the answer is 1, not 0.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The grid is nonempty and square; both endpoints must be open. Each move has the same cost and there are at most eight outgoing moves per cell. Therefore weighted shortest-path machinery is unnecessary. Distance counts cells including the start, so it is one larger than the number of moves.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The search permits a diagonal step whenever its destination is open; it does not require the two adjacent orthogonal cells to be open. That detail matters when the start is surrounded by blocked horizontal and vertical neighbours but has an open diagonal exit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fewest cells on a path from the top-left to the bottom-right corner through 0-cells, moving in 8 directions\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every move costs the same. Which search returns the fewest moves the first time it reaches a cell?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> BFS shortest paths\u003c/p>\u003cp>BFS from (0, 0) over open cells with all 8 directions, setting the distance when a cell is enqueued; the first time the bottom-right cell is reached, its distance (counting cells, so the start is 1) is the answer. Return -1 if either corner is blocked or the corner is never reached.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all simple valid paths from start to finish using backtracking and take the shortest. Even a small open grid can have exponentially many simple paths, and storing the current path takes O(n²) space. A visited mark local to each candidate is not enough to avoid repeated exploration across different candidates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use BFS with the start at distance 1. Its FIFO queue expands states in nondecreasing distance. When an open neighbour is first enqueued, its distance is one more than the current distance, and no shorter route can be found later: that route's predecessor would have been expanded earlier. Marking at enqueue makes each cell enter once. The first removal of the destination therefore gives the minimum number of cells.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">grid = [[0, 1, 0], [1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 0]]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue [(0, 0, 1)]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">From (0, 0), the open diagonal (1, 1) is discovered at distance 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue [(1, 1, 2)]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">(2, 2) is reached from (1, 1) at distance 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef shortest_path_binary_matrix(grid):\n    n = len(grid)\n    if grid[0][0] or grid[n - 1][n - 1]:\n        return -1\n    queue = deque([(0, 0, 1)])\n    seen = {(0, 0)}\n    while queue:\n        r, c, distance = queue.popleft()\n        if (r, c) == (n - 1, n - 1):\n            return distance\n        for dr in (-1, 0, 1):\n            for dc in (-1, 0, 1):\n                if dr == dc == 0:\n                    continue\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                if (0 &lt;= nr &lt; n and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; n and grid[nr][nc] == 0\n                        and (nr, nc) not in seen):\n                    seen.add((nr, nc))\n                    queue.append((nr, nc, distance + 1))\n    return -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are n² cells and at most eight checks for each expanded cell, so time and auxiliary space are O(n²). The grid is not modified; the seen set and queue hold the search state.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reject blocked start or finish before starting the search.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count the starting cell and include diagonal moves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mark cells on enqueue to prevent several parents from queuing the same cell.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A disconnected open destination must still return \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Record predecessors and return the actual shortest route.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If moves have unequal nonnegative costs, replace BFS with Dijkstra's algorithm.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For four-directional movement remove the diagonal offsets.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:994":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In a rectangular grid, \u003ccode>0\u003c/code> denotes an empty cell, \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> a fresh item and \u003ccode>2\u003c/code> a rotten item. Each minute every rotten item causes its fresh horizontal or vertical neighbours to rot simultaneously. Return the earliest minute when no fresh item remains, or \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code> if some fresh item never rots.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The nonempty grid may have several initial sources, none, or no fresh items. Effects spread through adjacent cells, not diagonally and not through empty cells. Because all sources act at once and every step costs one minute, the time for a cell is its shortest distance from any initial rotten cell through occupied cells. The code mutates the grid.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An unreachable fresh cell invalidates the whole result even if other fresh cells did rot.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Rot spreads from every rotten orange to its sideways neighbours once a minute; when is everything rotten?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>All the rotten oranges spread at the same time. What if they all start in the queue together?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Grid and multi-source BFS\u003c/p>\u003cp>Multi-source BFS seeded with every rotten orange at minute 0, one level per minute, counting fresh oranges as they rot. The answer is the number of levels that rotted something, or -1 if any fresh orange is left.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m n) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Make a copy of the grid at the start of each minute and scan every cell to decide which fresh neighbours will change for the next minute. With at most O(mn) productive minutes and O(mn) work per scan, this costs O((mn)²) time and O(mn) space. The snapshot is important: letting newly rotten cells spread immediately in the same scan would produce an incorrect time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start a single BFS with every initial rotten cell at time zero. All earlier-time cells are processed before later-time cells, so the first rot of a fresh neighbour is its earliest possible time from any source. Mark it rotten when enqueued, decrement the number of fresh cells, and assign its parent time plus one. If the queue empties with fresh cells remaining, no occupied route from any source can reach them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">grid = [[2, 1, 1], [1, 1, 0]]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sources [(0, 0)] at minute 0; fresh 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At minute 1: (0, 1), (1, 0) rot\u003c/p>\u003cpre>fresh 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At minute 2: (0, 2), (1, 1) rot\u003c/p>\u003cpre>fresh 0; return \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef oranges_rotting(grid):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    queue = deque()\n    fresh = 0\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] == 2:\n                queue.append((r, c, 0))\n            elif grid[r][c] == 1:\n                fresh += 1\n    elapsed = 0\n    while queue:\n        r, c, minute = queue.popleft()\n        for dr, dc in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 &lt;= nr &lt; rows and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; cols and grid[nr][nc] == 1:\n                grid[nr][nc] = 2\n                fresh -= 1\n                elapsed = minute + 1\n                queue.append((nr, nc, elapsed))\n    return elapsed if fresh == 0 else -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The initial scan and the search both take O(mn) time. Each cell is enqueued at most once and the queue can occupy O(mn) auxiliary space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Seed every initial source before starting; separate searches do not model simultaneous spread efficiently.\u003c/li>\u003cli>No fresh items means zero minutes, even when the queue is empty.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A fresh region isolated by empty cells produces \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Update a cell before enqueueing so two sources do not count it twice.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return each cell's earliest rot time instead of only the final minute.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use weighted edges and multi-source Dijkstra if transmission times vary.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stop after a fixed number of minutes and report the remaining fresh cells.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:417":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each cell in a rectangular height map can send water to a horizontally or vertically adjacent cell of no greater height. One ocean touches the top and left edges, the other the bottom and right edges. Return all coordinates from which water can reach both oceans, in any order. A border cell can reach its adjacent ocean without moving to another cell.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The height matrix is nonempty and rectangular. Equal heights allow movement in both directions. Many starting cells may share long drainage paths, so searching separately from every cell repeats work. Both oceans can touch the same cell, especially in a one-row or one-column grid; duplicates in border seeds must not cause duplicate output.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Water may take different routes to the two oceans; the routes do not need to be disjoint or to have the same length. Reaching an edge of the relevant ocean suffices, without traversing any additional cell representing the ocean itself.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Cells from which water, flowing to equal or lower neighbours, can reach both the top-left edges and the bottom-right edges\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Searching from every cell repeats the same work many times. Can you ask the question from the oceans instead?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Grid and multi-source BFS\u003c/p>\u003cp>Reverse the flow. From all Pacific border cells, search to neighbours whose height is at least the current cell's (uphill); do the same from the Atlantic border. A cell drains to an ocean exactly when that ocean's reverse search reaches it, so answer the intersection of the two reached sets.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m n) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each of the \u003ccode>mn\u003c/code> cells, run a downhill search to see which borders are reachable. A search can inspect O(mn) cells, yielding O((mn)²) time and O(mn) temporary space. It answers the right question but repeatedly traverses the same reachable regions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reverse every possible water edge. From an ocean border, move to a neighbour only if that neighbour is at least as high as the current cell. A reversed path from a border to a cell corresponds exactly to a valid downhill path from that cell to that border. Perform a multi-source traversal from each ocean's borders and intersect the two reached sets. This equivalence proves both inclusion directions, even across plateaus and around cycles.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">heights = [[1, 2], [4, 3]]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>top/left seeds {(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)}\nbottom/right seeds {(1, 0), (1, 1), (0, 1)}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">From the first ocean, (1, 1) is reachable uphill from (0, 1)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first reach set contains all four cells\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The second ocean cannot climb from its seeds to height 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>intersection \u003cmark>[(0, 1), (1, 0), (1, 1)]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def pacific_atlantic(heights):\n    rows, cols = len(heights), len(heights[0])\n\n    def reach(seeds):\n        seen = set(seeds)\n        stack = list(seen)\n        while stack:\n            r, c = stack.pop()\n            for dr, dc in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                if (0 &lt;= nr &lt; rows and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; cols\n                        and (nr, nc) not in seen\n                        and heights[nr][nc] &gt;= heights[r][c]):\n                    seen.add((nr, nc))\n                    stack.append((nr, nc))\n        return seen\n\n    first = [(0, c) for c in range(cols)] + [(r, 0) for r in range(rows)]\n    second = [(rows - 1, c) for c in range(cols)] + [(r, cols - 1) for r in range(rows)]\n    both = reach(first) &amp; reach(second)\n    return [(r, c) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols) if (r, c) in both]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each search visits at most mn cells and examines four neighbours, taking O(mn) time and space. The final row-major scan and the output each cost at most O(mn), so the total bounds remain O(mn) time and space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reverse flow climbs to equal or higher neighbours, not lower ones.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>&gt;\u003c/code>, to include level paths.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Include all four borders, and deduplicate corners and overlapping ocean borders.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A single cell is adjacent to both oceans and must be returned.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return cells reaching either ocean by taking a union instead of an intersection.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add more destinations and intersect one reverse-reachability set per destination.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Track one successor in each reverse search to reconstruct valid drainage paths.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:752":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A lock has four decimal wheels, initially showing \u003ccode>\"0000\"\u003c/code>. One move turns exactly one wheel one position forward or backward, wrapping between 0 and 9. Certain combinations are forbidden and cannot be entered. Find the fewest moves to a given target, or return \u003ccode>-1\u003c/code> when no legal route exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every combination is a four-character digit string, including leading zeroes. There are exactly 10,000 states and each has eight possible one-turn neighbours. All moves have equal cost, and a forbidden initial state prevents the search from starting, even when it equals the target.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The target may be blocked, isolated by blocked neighbours, or equal to the initial state. The first two cases fail, while the last needs zero turns provided the start is allowed. Keeping combinations as strings preserves the leading zeroes necessary to represent a particular wheel configuration.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fewest single-wheel turns to change a 4-digit lock from 0000 to a target while avoiding forbidden combinations\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>How many different combinations exist, and which combinations are one turn away from a given one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> BFS over a state space\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each of the 10^4 combinations is a vertex with 8 neighbours (one wheel up or down, wrapping between 9 and 0); BFS from \"0000\" with the dead ends pre-marked as visited gives the fewest turns. If \"0000\" is itself a dead end, return -1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(10^4 x 8) time, O(10^4) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate sequences of wheel turns of increasing length and simulate each, rejecting forbidden combinations. Without deduplicating states, many sequences lead back to the same combination and the branching factor is eight. This can take exponential time in the path length and may continue indefinitely unless an explicit bound is imposed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Treat combinations as graph vertices and turns as edges. BFS explores legal states in increasing number of moves. A state discovered for the first time has a shortest route: every shorter candidate would have an earlier-layer predecessor, which BFS already expanded. Record states when enqueued to keep each combination from being explored repeatedly. Forbidden combinations are excluded from the start.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">deadends = [], target = \"0009\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue [(\"0000\", 0)]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Turn the last wheel backward, wrapping 0 to 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>enqueue (\"0009\", 1)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The target is removed at distance 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef open_lock(deadends, target):\n    blocked = set(deadends)\n    if \"0000\" in blocked:\n        return -1\n    queue = deque([(\"0000\", 0)])\n    seen = {\"0000\"}\n    while queue:\n        state, distance = queue.popleft()\n        if state == target:\n            return distance\n        for i in range(4):\n            digit = int(state[i])\n            for step in (-1, 1):\n                next_digit = str((digit + step) % 10)\n                neighbor = state[:i] + next_digit + state[i + 1:]\n                if neighbor not in blocked and neighbor not in seen:\n                    seen.add(neighbor)\n                    queue.append((neighbor, distance + 1))\n    return -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At most 10⁴ states are visited, with eight constant-length neighbour constructions each. Time is O(10⁴ + D) and space is O(10⁴ + D), where D is the number of forbidden entries used to build the set.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Check a forbidden start before accepting a target equal to the start.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A forbidden target cannot be reached because it is never enqueued.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use modulo for both directions and retain all four digits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mark on enqueue rather than on removal to avoid duplicate work.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store predecessors to return a shortest sequence of combinations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use bidirectional BFS, expanding one frontier from each endpoint.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generalize to \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> wheels with \u003ccode>b\u003c/code> symbols, yielding at most b^k states.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:785":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An undirected graph is given as adjacency lists, with vertices numbered from zero. Determine whether its vertices can be split into two groups such that every edge connects different groups. Isolated vertices can go in either group. A triangle cannot be split this way, while an even cycle can.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The adjacency lists describe an undirected graph and can include disconnected components. A self-loop, if present, makes the condition impossible because its endpoints are the same vertex. The graph can be traversed in O(V + E) time, so testing all possible assignments is unnecessary.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Within one connected component, picking the opposite starting colour merely swaps the two groups; it cannot repair a conflict. Each disconnected component can make that arbitrary choice independently. An empty graph is also bipartite because there are no edges that could violate the condition.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can the vertices be split into two sets so that every edge goes between the sets?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Put one vertex on the left. Where must each of its neighbours go, and then their neighbours?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Cycle detection and two-colouring\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two-colour each component with BFS or DFS, giving each newly found vertex the opposite colour of the vertex that found it; an edge between equal colours means an odd cycle, so the answer is false. Start from every uncoloured vertex, because the graph may be disconnected.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(V + E) time, O(V) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assign each of V vertices one of two colours, and for each complete assignment check all edges. At most 2^V assignments and O(V + E) work per check yield O(2^V(V + E)) time. The search uses O(V) space for an assignment. The issue is trying assignments that are already forced by previously coloured neighbours.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose any colour for a new component's first vertex. Every edge forces its other endpoint to have the opposite colour; propagate this rule using a stack. If an edge joins equal colours, the current assignment violates a necessary condition, and the forced parity of paths means no valid colouring exists for that component. If no conflict appears, every examined edge connects opposite colours, so the resulting assignment itself proves success. Repeat from each still-uncoloured vertex.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">graph = [[1, 2], [0, 2], [0, 1]]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>colour vertex 0 as 0; its neighbours 1 and 2 as 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Expand vertex 2 and inspect edge 2-1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>both endpoints have colour 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">No two-group assignment can satisfy this triangle\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>False\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def is_bipartite(graph):\n    colors = [-1] * len(graph)\n    for start in range(len(graph)):\n        if colors[start] != -1:\n            continue\n        colors[start] = 0\n        stack = [start]\n        while stack:\n            u = stack.pop()\n            for v in graph[u]:\n                if colors[v] == -1:\n                    colors[v] = 1 - colors[u]\n                    stack.append(v)\n                elif colors[v] == colors[u]:\n                    return False\n    return True\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each vertex is coloured once and each adjacency entry is inspected once: O(V + E) time. Colours and the stack occupy O(V) auxiliary space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start a traversal in every uncoloured component, not just at vertex zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare against previously coloured neighbours instead of blindly overwriting them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An isolated vertex is valid, whereas a self-loop conflicts immediately.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Colour at discovery to avoid repeatedly pushing a vertex.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the two vertex groups when the colouring succeeds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Extract an odd cycle as a certificate when a conflict is found.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check whether a set of pairwise inequality constraints is consistent.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:210":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Courses are labelled \u003ccode>0..n-1\u003c/code>. Each pair \u003ccode>[course, prerequisite]\u003c/code> means the prerequisite must appear before the course. Return any ordering containing every course exactly once and respecting all pairs, or an empty list if no such order exists. With \u003ccode>[[1, 0], [2, 0]]\u003c/code>, both \u003ccode>[0, 1, 2]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[0, 2, 1]\u003c/code> work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The course count can be large relative to the number of pairs, and the dependencies need not connect all courses. A course can have several prerequisites, and there may be a directed cycle. Input pairs point opposite to the direction of scheduling influence: completing the prerequisite unlocks the course.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Courses with no relation still belong in the output, even if they can appear anywhere. A prerequisite cycle cannot be resolved by starting with an outside course: every member of that cycle still requires another member to appear earlier.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return an order in which to take all courses, given [course, prerequisite] pairs, or nothing if no order works\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which courses can you take straight away? After taking one, which others become available?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Topological sort\u003c/p>\u003cp>Kahn's algorithm with edges prerequisite -&gt; course: queue every course with in-degree 0, output a course when popped and decrement its dependents, queueing those that reach 0. If fewer than n courses come out, a cycle blocks the rest: return [].\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(V + E) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try each of the \u003ccode>n!\u003c/code> permutations and verify that every prerequisite precedes its course. Checking a candidate can take O(n + E) time with a position table, so the worst-case time is O(n!(n + E)); storing one candidate and its positions uses O(n) space. Most permutations are rejected because a single prerequisite is out of order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build edges from prerequisite to dependent and count the unsatisfied incoming edges of each course. Initially every zero-indegree course is eligible. When one is emitted, remove its outgoing edges and enqueue any dependent whose remaining count falls to zero. The invariant is that the count is exactly the number of prerequisite edges not yet removed. Thus every emitted course follows its prerequisites. If the queue empties before all courses appear, the remaining finite directed graph has no zero-indegree vertex and contains a cycle, making a complete ordering impossible.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 3, prerequisites = [[1, 0], [2, 0]]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>indegrees [0, 1, 1]; queue [0]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Emit 0 and remove 0-&gt;1 and 0-&gt;2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>indegrees [0, 0, 0]; queue [1, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Emit 1 then 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>order \u003cmark>[0, 1, 2]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef find_order(n, prerequisites):\n    outgoing = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    indegree = [0] * n\n    for course, prerequisite in prerequisites:\n        outgoing[prerequisite].append(course)\n        indegree[course] += 1\n    queue = deque(i for i in range(n) if indegree[i] == 0)\n    order = []\n    while queue:\n        u = queue.popleft()\n        order.append(u)\n        for v in outgoing[u]:\n            indegree[v] -= 1\n            if indegree[v] == 0:\n                queue.append(v)\n    return order if len(order) == n else []\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Constructing the graph and processing each vertex and edge once takes O(n + E) time and O(n + E) space, including the output and adjacency lists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reverse each input pair when building the outgoing adjacency list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check the output length: a partial order is not a valid complete answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Include independent courses, which start with indegree zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If duplicate pairs are supplied, count and remove every occurrence consistently.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use a min-heap of available courses to choose the lexicographically smallest order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return only whether completion is possible by comparing the processed count to \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add durations and compute earliest completion times while processing the DAG.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:802":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In a directed graph, a terminal vertex has no outgoing edges. A vertex is safe if every possible route starting there eventually reaches a terminal vertex. Return the safe vertices in increasing order. A vertex on a directed cycle is unsafe, and so is any vertex that can reach that cycle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input is an adjacency list with vertex labels \u003ccode>0..n-1\u003c/code>; disconnected regions are possible. Safety depends on \u003ci>all\u003c/i> outgoing choices, not merely the existence of one route to a terminal. A self-loop is a cycle. The result order is part of the contract, so a traversal's discovery order alone is insufficient.\u003c/p>\u003cp>In a finite graph an infinite route exists exactly when some reachable directed cycle can be followed repeatedly. Consequently a path that can choose either a terminal or a cycle is unsafe, because the adversarial choice can keep following the cycle forever.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Nodes from which every path eventually stops at a node with no outgoing edges\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Nodes with no outgoing edges are safe. Which other nodes become safe once you know that?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Topological sort\u003c/p>\u003cp>A node is safe when all of its successors are safe. Reverse the edges and run Kahn on out-degrees, starting from the terminal nodes: when a node's last unresolved successor is shown safe, it becomes safe too. Nodes on or leading into a cycle never reach out-degree 0. Return the safe nodes in increasing order.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(V + E) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each starting vertex, enumerate all possible paths until finding a cycle or confirming termination. Even in an acyclic graph the number of paths can be exponential due to branching and reconvergence. A separate cycle-detection traversal from every start is a more controlled O(V(V + E)) alternative, but still repeats work across starts; it uses O(V) search space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Terminal vertices are safe immediately. A nonterminal becomes safe once all its successors have been established safe. Reverse the edges and maintain each vertex's number of unresolved outgoing edges. Removing a known-safe successor decreases that number for each predecessor; when the count reaches zero, the predecessor is also safe. By induction every removed vertex is safe. Every vertex left behind has at least one outgoing edge to another remaining vertex, so following remaining edges eventually repeats a vertex and provides an infinite route. Thus all remaining vertices are unsafe.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">graph = [[1], [2], [], [3]]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>outdegrees [1, 1, 0, 1]; queue [2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove 2, then 1, then 0 as their last successors are resolved\u003c/p>\u003cpre>safe flags [True, True, True, False]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Vertex 3 retains its self-loop\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>[0, 1, 2]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef eventual_safe_nodes(graph):\n    n = len(graph)\n    reverse = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    remaining = [len(neighbors) for neighbors in graph]\n    for u, neighbors in enumerate(graph):\n        for v in neighbors:\n            reverse[v].append(u)\n    queue = deque(i for i in range(n) if remaining[i] == 0)\n    safe = [False] * n\n    while queue:\n        v = queue.popleft()\n        safe[v] = True\n        for u in reverse[v]:\n            remaining[u] -= 1\n            if remaining[u] == 0:\n                queue.append(u)\n    return [i for i in range(n) if safe[i]]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each vertex and adjacency entry is processed at most once, and the final ordered scan is O(V). Time and space are O(V + E), including reversed edges.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>One safe successor does not make a vertex safe if another leads to a cycle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use outdegree in the original graph, not indegree.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Scan labels in ascending order for the requested result.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Duplicate edges, if present, must both count and be removed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return unsafe vertices instead by complementing the safe flags.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Identify strongly connected components to describe the actual cyclic regions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Solve the same problem with three-colour DFS and memoized safety states.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:127":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You are given a starting word, a different ending word, and a dictionary of words of the same length. A transformation changes exactly one character, and every word after the starting word must belong to the dictionary. Return the smallest number of words in a valid chain, counting both endpoints; return zero if no chain exists. For example, \u003ccode>\"hit\"\u003c/code> can reach \u003ccode>\"cog\"\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>\"hot\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>\"dot\"\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>\"dog\"\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>\"cog\"\u003c/code>, giving five words.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The start is allowed to be absent from the dictionary. The end is not: reaching an unlisted end is invalid even if it differs from a dictionary word by one character. Reusing a word cannot improve a shortest chain, so a shortest solution does not need a cycle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>All words consist of lowercase English letters, have the same positive length L, and the dictionary contains N unique words. The start and end are distinct under the problem's contract. The input describes a potentially dense implicit graph: two words have an edge when exactly one position differs. Constructing every pair of edges explicitly can require O(N²L) comparisons and substantial space. Instead, group potential neighbours by the positions they agree on.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The special marker \u003ccode>*\u003c/code> is not a lowercase letter, so substituting it for one character creates an unambiguous pattern. Words with the same pattern at a position agree everywhere else. A pair of distinct dictionary words sharing that pattern differs in exactly one character. The code also includes the start in the index, since it is a legal starting vertex even when not listed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Shortest chain of words from a start word to an end word, changing one letter at a time and using only listed words\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Words are states and one-letter changes are moves. How can you find a word's neighbours without comparing it with every other word?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> BFS over a state space\u003c/p>\u003cp>BFS over words, counting words on the path. Group the words by wildcard patterns (h*t) so the neighbours of a word are the other words in its L buckets; return 0 if the end word is not listed. Searching from both ends at once is faster still.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(N L^2) time and space for N words of length L\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One approach is to enumerate simple transformation chains by backtracking, check each possible next word against the current word, and retain the shortest valid chain. The number of chains can be exponential or worse as the dictionary grows, despite using only O(N) space for a path and visited set. Even a BFS that compares a dequeued word with every dictionary word spends O(N²L) time in the worst case, repeatedly comparing pairs which cannot be neighbours.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Create L wildcard patterns per indexed word, placing the word in the bucket for each pattern. During BFS, the buckets of the current word list all its one-character neighbours. Assign each newly found word the current distance plus one, and mark it as visited at discovery. BFS processes words in nondecreasing chain length, so the first time the end is removed its length is minimal.\u003c/p>\u003cp>After processing a pattern, remove its bucket. This is safe because the first word that expands a bucket has minimum BFS distance among all words that could expand that bucket. Every undiscovered word in the bucket is then enqueued with the best possible distance through it; any later expansion of that same bucket could only offer an equal or longer route. Clearing buckets avoids repeatedly iterating a large group of mutually adjacent words. Visited words cannot hide a shorter route: their first discovery already came from a predecessor at no greater distance.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">begin = \"hit\", end = \"cog\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>words = [\"hot\", \"dot\", \"dog\", \"cog\"]\n    queue [(\"hit\", 1)]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pattern h*t joins hit to hot; then *ot joins hot to dot\u003c/p>\u003cpre>distances hit 1, hot 2, dot 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pattern do* joins dot to dog, then *og joins dog to cog\u003c/p>\u003cpre>distances dog 4, cog 5; return \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In this example each displayed pattern is consumed the first time it is expanded. The final length counts five words, while the number of character changes is four. The trace omits empty buckets, which do not affect the queue.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef ladder_length(begin, end, word_list):\n    words = set(word_list)\n    if end not in words:\n        return 0\n    words.add(begin)\n    length = len(begin)\n    buckets = defaultdict(list)\n    for word in words:\n        for i in range(length):\n            pattern = word[:i] + \"*\" + word[i + 1:]\n            buckets[pattern].append(word)\n    queue = deque([(begin, 1)])\n    seen = {begin}\n    while queue:\n        word, distance = queue.popleft()\n        if word == end:\n            return distance\n        for i in range(length):\n            pattern = word[:i] + \"*\" + word[i + 1:]\n            for neighbor in buckets.pop(pattern, []):\n                if neighbor not in seen:\n                    seen.add(neighbor)\n                    queue.append((neighbor, distance + 1))\n    return 0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let W be the number of distinct indexed words, at most N + 1. There are WL bucket memberships. Creating each length-L pattern using Python string slices and concatenation costs O(L), so preprocessing and searching cost O(WL²) time. Clearing each bucket ensures its memberships are scanned only once in total, O(WL), rather than once per word in that bucket. The pattern keys can occupy O(WL²) characters, and the membership lists, queue, and visited set use O(WL + W) additional references. Thus O(WL²) is a safe total auxiliary space bound for Python strings.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Check the end is in the dictionary before searching. A reachable-looking unlisted target is not allowed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count the start as length one; returning the number of edges is off by one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Include the start in the pattern index but do not require it in the dictionary.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mark discovered neighbours when enqueued and clear a bucket only after its full list has been considered.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The code expects the stated lowercase, equal-length input. A wildcard already present in an input word would invalidate the bucket interpretation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If all possible first changes are missing, the queue empties and the answer is zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Record all shortest-path predecessors, then backtrack to enumerate every shortest transformation sequence.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use bidirectional BFS, carefully preserving distances from both ends, to reduce the explored region.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generate 26 substitutions per position instead of indexing wildcard buckets, and check membership in a set.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2050":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> courses labelled from 1 to \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>; course \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> takes \u003ccode>time[i-1]\u003c/code> months. Each pair \u003ccode>[a, b]\u003c/code> requires completing \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> before starting \u003ccode>b\u003c/code>. Any number of eligible courses may run simultaneously. Return the minimum number of months by which every course can be completed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For example, with durations \u003ccode>[3, 2, 5]\u003c/code> and prerequisites \u003ccode>[[1, 3], [2, 3]]\u003c/code>, courses 1 and 2 can start together. Course 3 must wait until month 3 and then needs five months, so all work completes at month 8. Summing all durations would incorrectly serialize independent work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The course count is positive, durations are positive integers, labels are one-based, and the given prerequisite graph is a DAG. There is no limit on how many courses can run in parallel. Thus a course's earliest start is controlled by its latest-finishing prerequisite, not by the sum of prerequisite times. A disconnected course still contributes to the final completion time.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A DAG admits a topological ordering: for every edge, its source appears before its destination. Calculating finish times in this order means every prerequisite value is available when it is needed. There can be many valid orders, but the earliest finish of each course and the final answer are independent of which order is used.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Courses have durations and prerequisites, and any number can run at once; minimum months to finish them all\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When can a course start at the earliest? In what order can you compute that for every course?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Scheduling a DAG and its critical path\u003c/p>\u003cp>A course starts when its slowest prerequisite ends, so finish[c] = time[c] + max(finish[p] over its prerequisites p), computed in topological order (Kahn). The answer is the largest finish time: the length of the critical path.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + E) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One direct but expensive method enumerates all directed paths, totals the durations along each path, and returns the largest total. Every chain is a lower bound because its courses must run sequentially. In a branching and reconverging DAG the number of paths can be exponential, so listing them can require exponential time even though the input graph is small. A depth-first enumeration uses O(n) stack space for one current path, but repeatedly evaluates shared suffixes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An alternative exhaustive scheduler could try possible execution orders, but this introduces needless choices. Starting an available course earlier never delays another course when resources are unlimited, so no search over schedules is needed. The useful quantity is the longest weighted prerequisite path ending at each course.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each course \u003ccode>v\u003c/code>, let \u003ccode>finish[v]\u003c/code> be its earliest completion time. If it has no prerequisites, it can finish after its own duration. Otherwise, it must wait for \u003ci>all\u003c/i> immediate prerequisites, so \u003ccode>finish[v] = duration[v] + max(finish[u])\u003c/code> over all edges \u003ccode>u -&gt; v\u003c/code>. Process this recurrence using Kahn's topological algorithm.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Maintain a remaining-indegree count and a \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> value: the largest finish time among prerequisites processed so far. Initially courses with zero indegree enter the queue, and each ready value is zero. When popping \u003ccode>u\u003c/code>, its finish is \u003ccode>ready[u] + duration[u]\u003c/code>. Propagate this value to every dependent with a maximum update, decrement its indegree, and enqueue it when the last incoming edge has been processed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The invariant is that \u003ccode>ready[v]\u003c/code> is the maximum finish among already removed predecessors. When \u003ccode>v\u003c/code> enters the queue, all its predecessors have been removed, so the invariant gives its exact earliest start. Induction over the queue order proves each computed finish is attainable by starting courses at their earliest starts and necessary because no course can begin before any prerequisite ends. The maximum finish over all vertices is therefore both achievable and a lower bound for finishing everything. It is the weighted critical-path length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n = 3, relations = [[1, 3], [2, 3]], time = [3, 2, 5]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ready [0, 0, 0], indegree [0, 0, 2], queue [1, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Finish 1 at 3 and 2 at 2; propagate their maximum to course 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ready [0, 0, 3], indegree [0, 0, 0], queue [3]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Finish course 3 at 3 + 5 = 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>maximum finish \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The two prerequisites run concurrently; the smaller completion time does not delay course 3 past the larger one. The queue need not process 1 before 2: the maximum update is independent of that order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\ndef minimum_time(n, relations, time):\n    outgoing = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    indegree = [0] * n\n    for a, b in relations:\n        outgoing[a - 1].append(b - 1)\n        indegree[b - 1] += 1\n    ready = [0] * n\n    queue = deque(i for i in range(n) if indegree[i] == 0)\n    answer = 0\n    while queue:\n        u = queue.popleft()\n        finish = ready[u] + time[u]\n        answer = max(answer, finish)\n        for v in outgoing[u]:\n            ready[v] = max(ready[v], finish)\n            indegree[v] -= 1\n            if indegree[v] == 0:\n                queue.append(v)\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building adjacency lists and indegrees costs O(n + E), where E is the number of prerequisite pairs. Every course is queued once, and each relation is processed once, giving O(n + E) total time and O(n + E) auxiliary space. Python integers grow as necessary for large duration sums. The algorithm relies on the promised DAG; it does not report cycles.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Convert one-based relation labels to zero-based indices for the duration array.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a maximum, not a sum, when merging predecessor finish times.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Enqueue a dependent only after \u003ci>all\u003c/i> of its incoming edges have been handled.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With no relations the answer is the largest individual duration, not their sum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A long chain gives the sum of that chain's durations; a disconnected longer chain can determine the answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If adapting to arbitrary directed graphs, count processed vertices and explicitly report a cycle instead of returning a partial result.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store the maximizing predecessor for each course to reconstruct one critical path.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return every course's earliest start and finish rather than only the makespan.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a limit on simultaneous courses: the unlimited-resource critical-path recurrence alone no longer solves the scheduling problem.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
